/* Questions worth asking: run 3, shelf italy (collections 0-12) */
QA({"divine-comedy":[
["Why is it called a 'Comedy' if it goes through Hell?","In medieval usage a comedy began in misery and ended happily, in plain vernacular style; Dante called it simply 'Commedia', and the adjective 'Divina' was added by Boccaccio and first printed on a title page in Venice in 1555."],
["Who was Beatrice in real life?","Very probably Bice Portinari, daughter of the Florentine banker Folco Portinari, whom Dante first saw at nine; she married Simone de' Bardi and died in 1290, aged 24."],
["Why was Dante exiled from Florence?","As a White Guelph prior he opposed Pope Boniface VIII; when the Black Guelphs seized Florence in 1301 he was fined, then in 1302 condemned to be burned alive if he returned, and he never did."],
["Why does Virgil guide him and not someone else?","Virgil was the greatest Latin poet, author of the Aeneid, whose hero visits the underworld in Book 6; medieval readers also thought his Fourth Eclogue foretold Christ, making him the wisest of the unbaptized pagans."],
["Why can't Virgil take him into Paradise?","As a pagan who lived before Christ, Virgil belongs to Limbo; he vanishes at the top of Mount Purgatory in the Earthly Paradise, and Beatrice takes over, with St Bernard guiding the final ascent."],
["What is terza rima?","Dante's invented rhyme scheme of interlocking tercets, ABA BCB CDC, which pulls each stanza into the next; the poem has 100 cantos, 14,233 lines, built on the number three for the Trinity."],
["Who is at the very bottom of Hell?","Satan, three-faced and frozen to the waist in the lake of Cocytus, chews the three arch-traitors Judas, Brutus and Cassius, betrayers of Christ and of Julius Caesar."],
["Who are Paolo and Francesca?","Francesca da Rimini and her brother-in-law Paolo Malatesta, killed by her husband Gianciotto around 1285; in Inferno 5 she tells how reading the romance of Lancelot and Guinevere led them to their first kiss."],
["What is the story of Count Ugolino?","Ugolino della Gherardesca of Pisa was locked in a tower with his sons and grandsons in 1289 and starved; in Inferno 33 he gnaws the skull of Archbishop Ruggieri, who imprisoned him, and hints he ate his children."],
["Which line is written over the gate of Hell?","'Lasciate ogne speranza, voi ch'intrate', 'Abandon all hope, you who enter', the last line of the inscription at the start of Inferno 3."],
["What does 'contrapasso' mean?","The principle that each punishment mirrors the sin: the fortune-tellers walk with heads twisted backward, and the sowers of discord such as Mohammed are split open, as they split communities."],
["How does the whole poem end?","Dante gazes into God, sees three circles of light and a human image within them, and his will is turned like a wheel by 'l'amor che move il sole e l'altre stelle', the love that moves the sun and the other stars."],
["Did Dante put people he knew in Hell?","Yes: his teacher Brunetto Latini among the sodomites, Filippo Argenti, and a place reserved for Pope Boniface VIII, still alive in 1300, the poem's fictional date."]
],
"decameron":[
["What does 'Decameron' mean?","It is Boccaccio's coinage from Greek 'deka' (ten) and 'hēmera' (day), meaning 'ten days', because ten young people tell ten stories a day for ten days, making 100 tales."],
["What was the plague that sets the book in motion?","The Black Death, which hit Florence in spring 1348 and killed perhaps half or more of its roughly 100,000 people; Boccaccio's introduction describes mass graves and abandoned sick in grim detail."],
["Who tells the stories and where?","Seven women and three men, among them Pampinea, Fiammetta, Filomena, Panfilo, Filostrato and the irreverent Dioneo, meet in Santa Maria Novella and retire to villas in the hills near Fiesole."],
["Why are there only ten days of stories over a two-week stay?","They pause on Fridays and Saturdays for devotion and personal grooming, so the retreat lasts about fourteen days before they return to Florence."],
["What is the story of Griselda?","The last tale: the Marquis Gualtieri marries a peasant girl and tests her obedience by pretending to kill their children and divorcing her, then restores everything; Petrarch translated it into Latin and Chaucer retold it as the Clerk's Tale."],
["What is the tale of the falcon?","In Day 5, story 9, the impoverished Federigo degli Alberighi serves his last treasure, his prized falcon, as dinner to the widow Monna Giovanna, who had come to ask for it; moved, she later marries him."],
["What is the 'putting the devil back in hell' story?","Day 3, story 10, Dioneo's tale of the hermit Rustico who teaches the naive Alibech that sex is a holy way of putting the devil back in hell; translators long left it untranslated."],
["Why is it called the 'Human Comedy'?","Critics paired it with Dante's Divine Comedy because it portrays merchants, priests, wives and tricksters in this world rather than souls in the next; Francesco De Sanctis popularized the contrast."],
["Did Boccaccio regret writing it?","Late in life, influenced by a monk's warnings, he reportedly wanted to burn his vernacular works, and Petrarch talked him out of it; he spent his last years lecturing on Dante in Florence."],
["How did it influence English literature?","Chaucer borrowed its frame and several tales for The Canterbury Tales, and Shakespeare took the wager plot of Cymbeline and the heroine of All's Well That Ends Well from Decameron stories."],
["Has it been filmed?","Pier Paolo Pasolini's Il Decameron (1971) adapted nine tales with Neapolitan settings and won a Silver Bear in Berlin, opening his 'Trilogy of Life'."],
["What is a 'novella' in Boccaccio's sense?","From Italian 'novella', a piece of news, it meant a short prose tale of real-seeming events; his 100 novelle set the model for the European short story."]
],
"the-prince":[
["Who was The Prince written for?","Machiavelli first meant it for Giuliano de' Medici, then dedicated it to Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici, Duke of Urbino, hoping to win back a government job after the Medici returned in 1512."],
["Why was Machiavelli out of a job when he wrote it?","He had served the Florentine republic as secretary of the Second Chancery from 1498; when the Medici returned in 1512 he was dismissed, then imprisoned and tortured with the strappado in 1513 on suspicion of conspiracy."],
["Who is the model prince in the book?","Cesare Borgia, son of Pope Alexander VI, praised in chapter 7 for ruthlessly pacifying the Romagna, notably by having his governor Remirro de Orco cut in two in the piazza at Cesena."],
["Is it better to be loved or feared?","Chapter 17 says it is best to be both, but if you must choose it is safer to be feared, provided you avoid being hated, chiefly by leaving subjects' property and women alone."],
["What are virtù and fortuna?","Virtù is a ruler's energy, skill and boldness; fortuna is luck or circumstance, which in chapter 25 Machiavelli likens to a flooding river that can be dammed and, notoriously, to a woman who must be beaten."],
["Does Machiavelli actually say 'the end justifies the means'?","Not in those words; the nearest line in chapter 18 says that in judging princes people look at the result, 'si guarda al fine', so if a ruler wins and holds his state his means will be judged honourable."],
["What does the lion and the fox mean?","A prince must be a fox to spot traps and a lion to frighten wolves, since force alone and cunning alone each fail; this is in chapter 18, on keeping faith."],
["When was it published?","It circulated in manuscript from about 1513 and was printed only in 1532, five years after Machiavelli's death in 1527, with papal permission; the Church placed it on the Index of Forbidden Books in 1559."],
["How does The Prince end?","Chapter 26 is a passionate appeal to the Medici to free Italy from the 'barbarians', closing with lines from Petrarch's Italia mia promising that ancient Italian valour is not yet dead."],
["Why does 'Machiavellian' mean scheming?","Elizabethan drama turned him into a stage villain, the 'Machevill' who speaks the prologue to Marlowe's The Jew of Malta, and the adjective for cunning, unscrupulous politics dates from the late 16th century."],
["Was Machiavelli really a monarchist?","His longer Discourses on Livy praise republics like Rome as more stable and free, so readers from Spinoza to Rousseau argued The Prince was a warning or satire; most scholars see it as practical advice for a crisis."]
],
"courtier":[
["Where and when does The Book of the Courtier take place?","Over four evenings in March 1507 at the ducal palace of Urbino, where Duchess Elisabetta Gonzaga's circle debates what makes the perfect courtier; it was published in Venice in 1528."],
["What does 'sprezzatura' mean?","Castiglione's coinage for studied nonchalance, the art of hiding art so that difficult things look effortless; it comes from 'sprezzare', to scorn or hold cheap."],
["Who takes part in the conversations?","Real people, including Count Ludovico da Canossa, Federico Fregoso, Giuliano de' Medici, Pietro Bembo, later a cardinal, and Emilia Pia, who keeps the game going."],
["Who was Castiglione himself?","Baldassare Castiglione (1478-1529) was a diplomat for the courts of Urbino and Mantua and later papal nuncio to Spain, where he died in Toledo; Raphael's portrait of him hangs in the Louvre."],
["What does Book 3 argue about women?","Giuliano de' Medici defends the 'donna di palazzo', the court lady, arguing women are as capable of virtue and learning as men, against the misogynist jibes of Gasparo Pallavicino."],
["How does the book end?","Pietro Bembo gives a rapturous speech on Platonic love rising from beauty to the divine, and the company is startled to find dawn breaking over the mountain of Catria."],
["Why was it so influential in England?","Sir Thomas Hoby translated it in 1561, and it shaped Elizabethan ideals of the gentleman, from Sir Philip Sidney to the witty courtiers of Shakespeare's comedies."],
["What sports and skills should the courtier have?","He should be a good horseman, swordsman and wrestler, swim, play tennis, dance, sing, draw, know Latin and Greek, and do all of it with sprezzatura."],
["What is the opposite of sprezzatura?","Affectation, 'affettazione', the visible straining after effect, which Castiglione calls the vice to avoid above all others."],
["Is the Urbino of the book idealized?","Yes: written years later after Duke Guidobaldo and many speakers had died, it is partly a memorial to a vanished court, as the melancholy preface to Book 4 admits."]
],
"cellini":[
["Who was Benvenuto Cellini?","A Florentine goldsmith and sculptor (1500-1571), maker of the gold saltcellar for Francis I and the bronze Perseus with the Head of Medusa in the Loggia dei Lanzi, Florence."],
["How was the Perseus cast?","Cellini says that during the casting in 1549 his furnace faltered and the metal curdled, so he threw in all his household pewter, about 200 plates and pots, to keep the bronze flowing."],
["Did Cellini really kill people?","By his own account he killed several, including the murderer of his brother and a rival goldsmith named Pompeo, and he was pardoned by Pope Paul III, who said men unique in their art are above the law."],
["Did he claim to shoot the Constable of Bourbon?","Yes: he boasts that during the Sack of Rome in 1527 he shot Charles de Bourbon, commander of the imperial army, from the walls, and later manned cannon at Castel Sant'Angelo."],
["How did he escape from Castel Sant'Angelo?","Imprisoned in 1538 on a charge of stealing papal jewels, he climbed down on knotted bedsheets, fell and broke his leg, and was recaptured, later having visions in his cell."],
["What is the Cellini Salt Cellar?","A gold and enamel table piece made for Francis I of France around 1540-43, showing Neptune and Earth; stolen from Vienna's Kunsthistorisches Museum in 2003, it was recovered buried in a forest in 2006."],
["How was the autobiography written?","He dictated much of it to a young assistant while working, starting in 1558, and it circulated in manuscript until first printed in Naples in 1728."],
["Why do readers love it?","Its swaggering, boastful voice and vivid gossip about popes, dukes and artists make it one of the liveliest memoirs of the Renaissance; Goethe translated it into German."],
["Has Cellini been turned into opera or film?","Berlioz wrote the opera Benvenuto Cellini (1838) around the casting of the Perseus, and he appears in the film The Firebrand (1925) and its remake The Affairs of Cellini (1934)."],
["Was Cellini ever accused of sodomy?","Yes, he was convicted of sodomy in Florence in 1523 and again in 1557, serving four years under house arrest for the second, which is when he began dictating the memoir."]
]});
QA({"betrothed":[
["What does 'I promessi sposi' mean?","'The promised spouses', meaning an engaged couple; Renzo Tramaglino and Lucia Mondella are due to marry in a village on Lake Como in November 1628 when the wedding is forbidden."],
["Why can't Renzo and Lucia marry?","The local lord Don Rodrigo has bet his cousin Attilio that he can have Lucia, so he sends two bravi to threaten the parish priest Don Abbondio: 'questo matrimonio non s'ha da fare', this marriage is not to be performed."],
["What is a 'bravo'?","A hired armed thug in the pay of a nobleman in Spanish-ruled Lombardy, recognizable by a hairnet and long forelock; repeated official 'gride' (edicts) against them were ignored, as Manzoni mocks."],
["What is the trick of the surprise marriage?","Under canon law a marriage was valid if bride and groom declared themselves married before the priest and two witnesses, so Renzo and Lucia ambush Don Abbondio at night, but he throws a tablecloth over Lucia and escapes."],
["Who is the Nun of Monza, and was she real?","Gertrude, who shelters Lucia in a convent and then betrays her, is based on Marianna de Leyva, forced into a convent, who took a lover and was walled up for years after a 1607 trial."],
["Who is the Innominato?","'The Unnamed', a fearsome robber baron who kidnaps Lucia for Don Rodrigo, then after a sleepless night and her pleas is converted by Cardinal Federigo Borromeo and frees her; he is modelled on Francesco Bernardino Visconti."],
["What vow does Lucia make?","Terrified in the Innominato's castle, she vows to the Virgin to remain unmarried if she is saved; Fra Cristoforo later releases her from the vow in the plague hospital."],
["What historical events does the novel include?","The Milan bread riots of November 1628 on St Martin's Day, the passage of German Landsknechte during the War of the Mantuan Succession, and the plague of 1630 that killed perhaps half of Milan."],
["What are the 'untori'?","The 'anointers', people accused of spreading plague by smearing walls with poisonous ointment; Manzoni documents the hysteria and wrote a separate essay on the torture trial, the Storia della colonna infame."],
["How does the novel end?","Don Rodrigo dies of plague in the lazaretto, forgiven by Renzo; Renzo finds Lucia alive there, Fra Cristoforo dissolves her vow, and they marry and settle near Bergamo with children."],
["Why did Manzoni 'rinse his clothes in the Arno'?","He rewrote the 1827 version in Florentine Tuscan for the 1840-42 edition to create a shared national prose language, describing it as rinsing his rags in the Arno."],
["Why is it compulsory in Italian schools?","It became the founding prose text of the national language and Risorgimento culture; Verdi composed his Requiem in 1874 to mark Manzoni's death."]
],
"leopard":[
["What is 'the Leopard'?","The gattopardo, a serval or ocelot-like wild cat, appears on the coat of arms of the Salina family; it echoes the real Tomasi family crest, and the Prince calls his class 'leopards and lions' to be replaced by 'jackals and hyenas'."],
["What does 'Everything must change so that everything can stay the same' mean?","Tancredi's line to his uncle, 'Se vogliamo che tutto rimanga come è, bisogna che tutto cambi', argues the aristocracy must back Garibaldi to keep its power; 'gattopardismo' now means cosmetic reform."],
["Who was the real Prince of Salina?","Don Fabrizio is based on the author's great-grandfather Giulio Fabrizio Tomasi, Prince of Lampedusa, an amateur astronomer with his own observatory, just as the fictional Prince studies the stars and has asteroids named after his discoveries."],
["What historical event starts the plot?","Garibaldi's landing at Marsala on 11 May 1860 with about a thousand Redshirts, which led to the conquest of Sicily and its annexation to the Kingdom of Italy by plebiscite that October."],
["Why does Tancredi marry Angelica instead of Concetta?","Angelica, the beautiful daughter of the newly rich mayor Don Calogero Sedàra, brings money the penniless Tancredi needs, while his cousin Concetta, who loves him, has only lineage."],
["Why does Don Fabrizio refuse the Senate seat?","The Piedmontese envoy Chevalley offers him a senatorship, but he says Sicilians want sleep and oblivion, not change, and recommends Don Calogero instead."],
["What happens to the Prince's dog Bendicò?","The great Dane is stuffed after death and kept for decades; in the final chapter, set in 1910, Concetta has the moth-eaten body thrown out of the window, a final image of the vanished world."],
["How does Don Fabrizio die?","In a hotel room in Palermo in July 1883, surrounded by his family, he reckons up his life and sees a young woman, Death, come for him."],
["Why was the novel rejected before publication?","Mondadori and Einaudi turned it down, with Elio Vittorini's verdict proving especially notorious; Lampedusa died in 1957 and Giorgio Bassani got it published by Feltrinelli in 1958."],
["What prize did it win?","The Strega Prize in 1959, and became a huge bestseller, though some left-wing critics attacked it as nostalgic and reactionary."],
["Who made the film?","Luchino Visconti's 1963 film starred Burt Lancaster, Alain Delon and Claudia Cardinale, won the Palme d'Or, and is famous for its 45-minute ball scene; Netflix released a series in 2025."]
],
"zeno":[
["What does 'La coscienza di Zeno' mean?","'Coscienza' means both conscience and consciousness in Italian, so the title captures both Zeno Cosini's guilty self-justifications and his stream of self-awareness."],
["Why does Zeno write the book?","His psychoanalyst, 'Dr S.', asks him to write his memories as therapy; the doctor publishes them out of spite because Zeno has abandoned treatment, as he explains in a preface."],
["What is the 'last cigarette'?","Zeno keeps resolving to smoke his last cigarette, marking dates like 'L.S.' on walls and books; the joke is that each 'last' one tastes best, so he never quits."],
["How does Zeno end up married to the wrong sister?","He courts the beautiful Ada Malfenti, who rejects him, then proposes to Alberta, who also refuses, and finally to the cross-eyed Augusta the same evening, who accepts; the marriage turns out happy."],
["What happens at Guido's funeral?","Zeno, having frantically speculated to rescue the bankrupt Guido's family on the stock exchange, follows the wrong funeral procession and misses his brother-in-law's burial."],
["How does the novel end?","In 1916, Zeno declares himself cured because he has got rich trading in wartime, and imagines a man inventing an explosive that will blow up the earth, curing it of illness."],
["Who was Italo Svevo?","The pen name, 'Italus the Swabian', of Aron Hector Schmitz (1861-1928), a Triestine businessman in his wife's family's marine paint firm, whose two earlier novels flopped."],
["What did James Joyce have to do with Svevo?","Joyce taught Svevo English in Trieste around 1907, admired his work, and after Zeno appeared in 1923 promoted it in Paris, leading French critics to launch Svevo's fame."],
["Was Svevo himself psychoanalysed?","No, though his brother-in-law was treated by Freud and Svevo read and translated Freud; the novel is skeptical, even mocking, about analysis."],
["How did Svevo die?","He died in September 1928 after a car accident at Motta di Livenza; on his deathbed he reportedly asked for a cigarette, adding that it would be his last."]
],
"periodic-table":[
["Why does each chapter have a chemical element's name?","Primo Levi gives 21 chapters the names of elements, from Argon to Carbon, each linked to an episode or person in his life, like iron for his climbing friend Sandro Delmastro."],
["Why is the first chapter called 'Argon'?","Argon is an inert gas, from Greek 'argos', idle; Levi compares it to his Piedmontese Jewish ancestors, quietly apart from society, and records their Hebrew-Piedmontese dialect."],
["What happens in the 'Vanadium' chapter?","After the war Levi, working for a paint company, finds a German supplier's chemist named Müller was a civilian supervisor in the Auschwitz lab; they exchange letters, but Müller dies before they can meet."],
["What is the 'Cerium' story?","In Auschwitz Levi and his friend Alberto stole cerium rods from the lab and filed them into lighter flints to trade for bread, which helped them survive."],
["What is the final chapter 'Carbon' about?","It follows an imaginary carbon atom from limestone through air, a vine leaf and a glass of wine to a cell in Levi's brain, ending with the dot he is writing on the page."],
["Who was Sandro in 'Iron'?","Sandro Delmastro, Levi's fellow chemistry student and mountain guide, who joined the partisans in the Action Party and was shot by a teenage fascist in 1944 while trying to escape."],
["What did the Royal Institution say about the book?","In 2006 the Royal Institution of Great Britain voted it the best science book ever written, ahead of works by Darwin and others."],
["Why was Levi a chemist at all?","He graduated in chemistry from Turin in 1941 despite Racial Laws, and his chemical skill got him work in the Buna lab at Auschwitz, which he believed saved his life."],
["How did Primo Levi die?","He fell down the stairwell of his Turin apartment building on 11 April 1987; it was ruled suicide, though some friends and biographers dispute that."],
["What does 'Zinc' teach?","Levi observes that pure zinc resists acid but impure zinc reacts readily, using it as a parable in praise of impurity and diversity against the Fascist cult of purity."]
],
"family-lexicon":[
["What does 'Lessico famigliare' mean?","'Family lexicon' or 'family sayings', the private phrases of the Levi household in Turin, such as her father's insults 'sempio' and 'negro', meaning a clumsy or ill-mannered person."],
["Who was Natalia Ginzburg's father?","Giuseppe Levi, a famous anatomist at the University of Turin, who taught three future Nobel laureates, Rita Levi-Montalcini, Salvador Luria and Renato Dulbecco."],
["What happened to her husband Leone Ginzburg?","Leone, a Russian-born scholar and anti-fascist co-founder of the publisher Einaudi, was arrested in Rome and died in Regina Coeli prison in February 1944 after torture by the Germans."],
["Why is the book so unemotional about tragedy?","Ginzburg deliberately records events in her family's offhand tone, so Leone's death is stated in a line or two, and grief appears through omission rather than lament."],
["Who are the famous people in the book?","Friends include Adriano Olivetti, who helped smuggle the socialist leader Filippo Turati to France in 1926, and the writer Cesare Pavese, whose 1950 suicide is recorded."],
["Why did the Levi family face persecution?","The father was Jewish and the family anti-fascist; the brothers were arrested for anti-fascist activity, and the 1938 Racial Laws pushed the father out of his job and into exile in Belgium."],
["What prize did it win?","It won the Strega Prize, Italy's most prestigious literary award, in 1963, the year it was published by Einaudi."],
["What does 'Senti questa' signal?","It was the mother's habitual opener, 'listen to this', and such repeated phrases become a code by which the scattered siblings recognize one another years later."],
["Was Ginzburg involved in politics later?","Yes, she was elected to the Italian parliament in 1983 as an independent on the Communist list and served until her death in 1991."]
],
"invisible-cities":[
["What is the setup of Invisible Cities?","Marco Polo describes to the aging emperor Kublai Khan 55 cities supposedly in his empire, each with a woman's name, such as Zaira, Despina and Zobeide, interspersed with their dialogues."],
["Why do the cities have women's names?","Calvino gave them names like Isidora, Anastasia and Octavia, many from classical or literary sources, to stress that they are imagined, desired places rather than real ones."],
["Are all the cities really Venice?","Polo admits that every time he describes a city he says something about Venice, which he never names because he fears losing it by speaking of it."],
["How is the book structured?","Nine chapters with 55 city descriptions in eleven thematic series, such as 'Cities and Memory' and 'Thin Cities', arranged in a mathematical pattern of entries per chapter."],
["What is Octavia, the spiderweb city?","A city suspended on a net of ropes between two mountains above an abyss, whose inhabitants know the net will last only so long."],
["How does the book end?","Polo says the inferno of the living is already here, and there are two ways to escape suffering it: accept it, or seek out and give space to who and what, in the midst of inferno, are not inferno."],
["What is the chessboard conversation?","Kublai reduces his empire to a chessboard, but Polo reads the grain of a square of wood, showing how a tiny piece holds the history of a tree, a forest and a craftsman."],
["When was it published, and what was Calvino involved in?","It appeared in 1972, while Calvino lived in Paris and belonged to the Oulipo, the group of writers using mathematical constraints, which shaped the book's structure."],
["Why do architects love it?","Its imagined cities about memory, desire and signs became a touchstone in architecture and urban planning schools, and it inspired many art exhibitions and design projects."],
["Did Calvino win the Nobel?","No; he was considered a leading candidate but died of a cerebral hemorrhage in Siena in September 1985, aged 61."]
]});
QA({"canzoniere":[
["What does 'Canzoniere' mean, and what did Petrarch call it?","'Songbook'; Petrarch's own Latin title was Rerum vulgarium fragmenta, 'Fragments of vernacular matters', a modest name for 366 poems he revised for decades."],
["Who was Laura?","A woman Petrarch says he first saw in the church of Sainte-Claire in Avignon on 6 April 1327, Good Friday; she is traditionally identified with Laura de Noves, wife of Hugues de Sade, though this is unproven."],
["How did Laura die?","Petrarch noted she died on 6 April 1348, exactly 21 years after their first meeting, during the Black Death, and the collection divides into poems 'in life' and 'in death' of Laura."],
["Why 366 poems?","One for each day of the year plus an opening sonnet, a deliberate symbolic number, ending with a hymn to the Virgin Mary."],
["What puns are hidden in Laura's name?","Petrarch plays on lauro (laurel, the poet's crown), l'aura (the breeze) and l'auro (gold), linking his beloved to fame and poetry."],
["What is a Petrarchan sonnet?","Fourteen lines split into an octave rhyming ABBAABBA and a sestet such as CDECDE, with a turn, or volta, between them; Wyatt and Surrey brought it into English."],
["What are 'Petrarchan conceits'?","Elaborate paradoxes and comparisons, like freezing fire or a lady's eyes as stars, that became clichés Shakespeare mocked in Sonnet 130, 'My mistress' eyes are nothing like the sun'."],
["Why was Petrarch crowned poet laureate?","He was crowned with laurel on the Capitoline Hill in Rome on 8 April 1341, the first such crowning since antiquity, mainly for his Latin works."],
["Is Petrarch the father of humanism?","He is often called that, because he hunted down lost classical manuscripts, such as Cicero's letters to Atticus, found at Verona in 1345, and championed reviving ancient learning."],
["What is the ascent of Mont Ventoux?","Petrarch's famous letter describes climbing the mountain in 1336 with his brother, opening Augustine's Confessions at the summit, and turning inward; it is often called the first mountaineering for pleasure."],
["What is 'Italia mia'?","Canzone 128, a lament over Italy torn by war and mercenaries, whose closing lines Machiavelli quoted to end The Prince."]
],
"orlando-furioso":[
["What does 'Orlando Furioso' mean?","'Orlando gone mad', or 'Roland Raving'; Orlando is the Italian name of Roland, Charlemagne's paladin from the Song of Roland."],
["Is it a sequel to something?","Yes: it continues Matteo Maria Boiardo's unfinished Orlando Innamorato (Orlando in Love), picking up the story of the knights' wars against the Saracens and Orlando's passion for the princess Angelica."],
["Why does Orlando go mad?","He discovers carvings and hears that Angelica, whom he adores, has married the humble Saracen soldier Medoro; he strips off his armour, uproots trees and rampages naked across France and Africa."],
["How is Orlando's sanity restored?","The English knight Astolfo flies the hippogriff to the Earthly Paradise, then rides Elijah's chariot with St John to the Moon, where all lost things are kept, and brings back Orlando's wits in a bottle."],
["What is a hippogriff?","A winged horse with an eagle's front and head, born of a griffin and a mare, which Ariosto made famous; J.K. Rowling's Buckbeak descends from it."],
["Who are Ruggiero and Bradamante?","The Saracen knight Ruggiero and the Christian warrior-woman Bradamante are lovers whose marriage, after his baptism, ends the poem and founds the House of Este, Ariosto's patrons."],
["How does the poem end?","Ruggiero kills the Saracen king Rodomonte in a duel at his wedding feast, the final lines describing Rodomonte's soul fleeing cursing to the underworld."],
["What is ottava rima?","An eight-line stanza rhyming ABABABCC, used by Ariosto for 46 cantos and nearly 39,000 lines; Byron used it for Don Juan."],
["Who was Ariosto's patron, and what did he say about the poem?","Cardinal Ippolito d'Este, to whom it was dedicated, supposedly asked, 'Messer Ludovico, where did you find so much nonsense?'"],
["When was it published?","The first edition came out in Ferrara in 1516 in 40 cantos; the final version of 46 cantos appeared in 1532, a year before Ariosto died."],
["What is Angelica and the Orc?","A famous episode in which Angelica is chained naked on a rock as a sacrifice to a sea monster, the orc, and rescued by Ruggiero on the hippogriff, painted by Ingres."],
["What did it inspire?","Handel's operas Orlando, Ariodante and Alcina, Vivaldi's Orlando furioso, Spenser's Faerie Queene, and Shakespeare's Much Ado About Nothing, which borrows the Ariodante and Ginevra plot."]
],
"vasari":[
["Who was Giorgio Vasari?","A painter and architect from Arezzo (1511-1574), who designed the Uffizi in Florence and the corridor named after him, and wrote the first great history of Italian art."],
["When was the Lives published?","First in Florence in 1550, then in a greatly enlarged second edition in 1568 with woodcut portraits; the second edition added living artists, including Vasari himself."],
["Did Vasari invent the word 'Renaissance'?","Not the word itself, but he spoke of the 'rinascita', the rebirth of art after the Middle Ages, which 19th-century historians turned into the Renaissance."],
["Where does the term 'Gothic' come from?","Vasari used 'maniera tedesca' and blamed the Goths for medieval architecture he considered barbaric, a scornful label that became the neutral term 'Gothic'."],
["What is the story of Giotto's O?","Vasari says that when a papal envoy asked for a drawing sample, Giotto drew a perfect circle freehand in red paint, proving his skill, whence the proverb 'rounder than Giotto's O'."],
["Did Michelangelo's nose get broken?","Vasari and others relate that the sculptor Pietro Torrigiano punched Michelangelo in a quarrel while drawing in the Brancacci Chapel, flattening his nose for life."],
["Who is the hero of the Lives?","Michelangelo, whom Vasari calls divine and the culmination of art; his biography is the longest and ends the second edition's story of progress."],
["Is the Lives reliable?","It mixes careful observation with anecdote, Florentine bias and errors, such as the tale that Andrea del Castagno murdered Domenico Veneziano, who in fact outlived him."],
["What did Vasari say about the Mona Lisa?","He described the portrait of Lisa del Giocondo in detail, including her eyebrows, and claimed Leonardo hired musicians and jesters to keep her smiling during sittings."],
["Did Vasari paint over a lost Leonardo?","A theory suggests his 1563 fresco in the Palazzo Vecchio covers Leonardo's lost Battle of Anghiari, because of a flag reading 'cerca trova' ('seek and you shall find'), but nothing has been proved."]
],
"casanova":[
["Who was Giacomo Casanova?","A Venetian adventurer (1725-1798) who was, in turn, a cleric, soldier, violinist, spy, gambler, lottery promoter and librarian, best known for his amorous memoirs."],
["Why did Casanova write his memoirs in French?","French was the language of the international elite of his day, and he wrote Histoire de ma vie in the 1790s as librarian at Count Waldstein's castle at Dux in Bohemia."],
["How did Casanova escape from the Leads prison?","Imprisoned in 1755 in the 'Piombi' under the lead roof of the Doge's Palace, he and a monk, Father Balbi, broke through the ceiling in 1756, crossed the roofs and walked out through the palace."],
["Why was he imprisoned?","He was arrested by the Venetian Inquisitors for 'irreligion', freemasonry, magical practices and libertinism, without a trial, and sentenced to five years."],
["Did Casanova really start the French lottery?","He helped launch the state lottery in Paris in 1757, which made him rich for a time and gave him entry to society."],
["How many women did he claim?","His memoirs describe about 120 to 130 lovers, a large but not fantastical number, including the nun M.M. and the mysterious Henriette."],
["Who was Henriette?","A refined Frenchwoman he met disguised as an officer in 1749 and lived with in Parma; she left him, and he later found the words she had scratched on a window: 'You will forget Henriette too'."],
["When was the manuscript published?","Bowdlerized versions appeared from 1822; the full original text was only published in 1960-62, and in 2010 the French National Library bought the manuscript for over 7 million euros."],
["Did Casanova meet famous people?","He met Voltaire, Rousseau, Catherine the Great, Frederick the Great, Benjamin Franklin and Pope Clement XIII, and reportedly helped Da Ponte with Don Giovanni."],
["Has Casanova been filmed?","Fellini's Casanova (1976) with Donald Sutherland, and the 2005 film with Heath Ledger, are among many versions; his name has become a byword for a seducer."]
],
"leopardi":[
["Who was Giacomo Leopardi?","A poet and philosopher from Recanati in the Marche (1798-1837), a prodigy who taught himself Greek and Hebrew in his father's library and suffered lifelong ill health."],
["What is 'L'infinito'?","A 15-line poem from 1819 in which the poet, sitting behind a hedge on a hill, imagines endless spaces and silences, ending 'e il naufragar m'è dolce in questo mare', sweet shipwreck in this sea."],
["Where is the hill of 'L'infinito'?","Monte Tabor, a small hill near the Leopardi palace in Recanati, now called the Colle dell'Infinito and a pilgrimage site."],
["What does 'Canti' mean?","'Songs'; the collection first appeared under that title in Florence in 1831, was expanded in Naples in 1835, and reached its final 41 poems in the posthumous 1845 edition."],
["Who was Silvia in 'A Silvia'?","Teresa Fattorini, daughter of the Leopardi family's coachman, who died of tuberculosis in 1818 at about 21; her singing at the window is the poem's central memory."],
["What is Leopardi's 'pessimism'?","A philosophy that nature is a cruel stepmother indifferent to humans and happiness is an illusion, set out in poems and the dialogues of his Operette morali."],
["What is 'La ginestra'?","His last great poem, 'The Broom', set on the slopes of Vesuvius, in which the humble desert plant becomes a symbol of human solidarity against an indifferent nature."],
["How did Leopardi die?","He died in Naples in June 1837, aged 38, during a cholera epidemic, possibly of heart and lung disease; his friend Antonio Ranieri had him buried, now at the Parco Virgiliano."],
["What is the Zibaldone?","Leopardi's huge notebook of thoughts, about 4,500 pages written from 1817 to 1832, published only in 1898-1900 and translated into English in full in 2013."],
["Is there a film about him?","Mario Martone's Il giovane favoloso (2014), starring Elio Germano, dramatizes his life from Recanati to Naples."]
]});
QA({"six-characters":[
["What happens at the start of Six Characters in Search of an Author?","A theatre company is rehearsing Pirandello's own play The Rules of the Game when six characters, abandoned by the author who created them, walk in and demand that the Director stage their story."],
["Who are the six characters?","The Father, the Mother, the Stepdaughter, the Son, and two silent children, a Boy and a Little Girl, plus Madame Pace, who appears as if summoned by her hat shop's props."],
["What is the shameful scene at Madame Pace's?","The Father, visiting the back room of Madame Pace's dress shop, a cover for a brothel, nearly has sex with the Stepdaughter, whom he does not recognize, until the Mother bursts in."],
["How does the play end?","The Little Girl drowns in the garden fountain and the Boy shoots himself with a revolver; the actors argue whether it is real or pretend, and the Director cries that he has lost a whole day."],
["Why did the premiere cause a riot?","At Rome's Teatro Valle on 9 May 1921, spectators shouted 'Manicomio!' ('Madhouse!'), fights broke out, and Pirandello had to leave by a side door, but the 1922 Milan revival was a triumph."],
["What is 'teatro nel teatro'?","Theatre within the theatre, or metatheatre: Pirandello breaks the fourth wall by making the play about staging a play, a device later used by Brecht, Beckett and Stoppard."],
["What is 'pirandelliano'?","An Italian adjective, like 'Pirandellian' in English, for situations where reality, identity and illusion become confusingly entangled."],
["When did Pirandello win the Nobel?","In 1934, 'for his bold and ingenious revival of dramatic and scenic art'."],
["What was Pirandello's relationship with Fascism?","He joined the Fascist Party in 1924, months after the murder of the socialist Giacomo Matteotti, and received state support for his Teatro d'Arte, though he later grew disillusioned."],
["How did Pirandello's own family life shape his work?","His wife Antonietta became mentally ill after his father's sulphur mine flooded in 1903 ruining the family, and her paranoid jealousy fed his themes of madness and multiple selves; she was committed in 1919."]
],
"moon-bonfires":[
["What are the bonfires of the title?","The 'falò' are bonfires lit on the hills of the Langhe in Piedmont on St John's Eve to bring rain and good harvests; at the end they recall the fire that burned Santina's body."],
["Who is the narrator?","Anguilla, 'Eel', a foundling raised by poor peasants who emigrated to America and grew rich, and returns after the war to his Piedmontese village."],
["Why is he called Anguilla?","It is a nickname meaning eel, suggesting someone slippery who slips away, as he did by emigrating, and a rootless creature with no known parents."],
["Who is Nuto?","Anguilla's older friend, a carpenter and clarinettist who stayed in the village, sympathized with the partisans and acts as his guide to what happened during the war."],
["What happened to Santina?","The youngest daughter of the landowner Anguilla once worked for, she spied for the Fascists and the partisans, was shot as a traitor by the partisans, and her body was burned 'like a bonfire'."],
["What happens to the boy Cinto?","Cinto, a lame boy who reminds Anguilla of himself, survives when his father Valino murders his family and burns the farmhouse, and Nuto takes him in as an apprentice."],
["When was the novel published, and what happened to Pavese soon after?","It appeared in April 1950; in August that year Pavese, having just won the Strega Prize for La bella estate, killed himself with sleeping pills in a Turin hotel room."],
["What were Pavese's last written words?","He wrote in his copy of Dialogues with Leucò: 'Perdono tutti e a tutti chiedo perdono', I forgive everyone and ask everyone's forgiveness, adding a plea not to gossip too much about his death."],
["What does the moon have to do with farming in the book?","Nuto insists peasants must sow, prune and cut wood by the phases of the moon, folk wisdom Anguilla doubts but comes to respect."],
["How was Pavese linked to American literature?","He translated Moby-Dick, Sinclair Lewis and Steinbeck into Italian in the 1930s, helping create Italian enthusiasm for American writing."]
],
"day-owl":[
["What is the Day of the Owl about?","Captain Bellodi, a carabinieri officer from Parma, investigates the shooting of the builder Salvatore Colasberna in a Sicilian town square, uncovering a Mafia web linking contractors and politicians."],
["Why is it called The Day of the Owl?","The title comes from Henry VI, Part 3, quoted as an epigraph: 'as the owl by day', a bird out of place in daylight, like the Mafia once exposed."],
["Why was Colasberna killed?","He refused to accept the Mafia's 'protection' and to rig building tenders, so he was shot in the square as he boarded the bus to Palermo."],
["What is 'omertà'?","The Mafia code of silence, under which witnesses, like the passengers who saw Colasberna shot, claim to have seen nothing."],
["Who is Don Mariano Arena?","The local Mafia boss Bellodi arrests, who divides humanity into 'men, half-men, little men, arse-lickers and quacks', and pays Bellodi a backhanded compliment by calling him a man."],
["How does the novel end?","The suspects' alibis are confirmed by powerful friends in Rome, the case collapses, and Bellodi, transferred to Parma, vows to return to Sicily even if he breaks his neck."],
["Why was the book significant in 1961?","At a time when officials still denied the Mafia existed, Sciascia depicted its links to politics openly, making it one of the first Italian novels about the Mafia."],
["Was Sciascia a politician?","Yes: he was a Communist city councillor in Palermo in 1975, then a Radical Party deputy from 1979 and served on the commission investigating the Moro murder."],
["What was the 'professionals of anti-Mafia' controversy?","In 1987 Sciascia wrote an article criticizing anti-Mafia careerists, including the judge Paolo Borsellino's appointment, which caused outrage; Borsellino was killed by the Mafia in 1992."],
["Was it filmed?","Damiano Damiani's Il giorno della civetta (1968) starred Franco Nero as Bellodi and Claudia Cardinale as the widow Rosa Nicolosi."]
],
"history-morante":[
["What is History about?","Ida Ramundo, a widowed Roman schoolteacher with a secret Jewish mother, is raped by a drunk German soldier in 1941 and bears a son, Useppe, whom she raises through wartime bombing and hunger."],
["Who is Useppe?","Giuseppe, nicknamed Useppe, the epileptic, unusually joyful child of the rape, who loves animals and nature, especially the sheepdog Bella."],
["Who is Nino?","Ida's older son Ninnarieddu, a reckless boy who goes from Fascist youth to partisan to black marketeer, and is killed in 1946 fleeing police while smuggling."],
["What is Davide Segre's story?","A Jewish anarchist and partisan who lost his family to the camps, becomes a friend of Nino and Useppe, and dies of a drug overdose in a slum."],
["How does the novel end?","Useppe dies of an epileptic fit in 1947, Ida goes mad and spends nine years in an asylum, and the final words note that 'history continues'."],
["Why does each chapter start with a timeline?","Morante opens each year with a list of world events in small type, contrasting 'History' of wars and statesmen with the suffering of anonymous people."],
["Why was the book controversial in 1974?","It sold 600,000 copies in a year, partly because Morante insisted on a cheap paperback, but left-wing critics attacked it as sentimental and politically naive."],
["What is the San Lorenzo bombing?","The Allied raid on Rome's San Lorenzo district on 19 July 1943, which killed about 3,000 people and destroyed Ida's home; it was the first bombing of Rome."],
["What does the epigraph 'Por el analfabeto a quien escribo' mean?","A line from César Vallejo, 'For the illiterate person I write for', signalling that Morante wanted the novel to reach ordinary readers."],
["Who was Elsa Morante married to?","Alberto Moravia, from 1941; they separated in 1962 but never divorced."]
],
"name-rose":[
["What is the meaning of the title The Name of the Rose?","It comes from the last line, 'Stat rosa pristina nomine, nomina nuda tenemus', 'The rose of old remains only in its name; we hold only bare names', adapted from Bernard of Cluny's 12th-century poem."],
["Who is William of Baskerville named after?","His surname nods to Conan Doyle's The Hound of the Baskervilles, and his reasoning to William of Ockham, the Franciscan philosopher famous for 'Ockham's razor'."],
["Who is Jorge of Burgos, and who is he based on?","The blind, fanatical old librarian monk is a tribute to the blind Argentine writer Jorge Luis Borges, whose story The Library of Babel inspired the labyrinth."],
["Why are the monks dying?","Jorge has poisoned the pages of the only copy of the lost second book of Aristotle's Poetics, on comedy; monks who lick their fingers to turn the pages die."],
["Why does Jorge want to hide Aristotle on comedy?","He fears that if Aristotle legitimized laughter, people would lose their fear of the Devil and God, undermining Church authority."],
["How does the novel end?","Jorge eats the poisoned pages and knocks over a lamp, and the whole library and abbey burn for three days; William admits that he found the truth by mistake."],
["When is it set, and what is the historical background?","In November 1327 in a Benedictine abbey in northern Italy, during the dispute over apostolic poverty between the Franciscans, Emperor Louis IV and Pope John XXII in Avignon."],
["Who was Bernard Gui?","A real Dominican inquisitor (c. 1261-1331), author of a manual for inquisitors, who in the novel condemns the cellarer Remigio, the hunchback Salvatore and the peasant girl to the stake as heretics."],
["What does 'penitenziagite' mean?","A garbled Latin cry of the hunchback Salvatore, mixing 'penitentiam agite', 'repent', with Italian, a slogan of the heretical Dolcinians."],
["Who were the Dolcinians?","Followers of Fra Dolcino, a radical apostolic preacher burned in 1307 after a crusade against his followers in the Piedmontese mountains; the cellarer Remigio was once one of them."],
["Who is the narrator Adso?","Adso of Melk, a young Benedictine novice from Austria, recalls the events as an old man; his only sexual encounter, with a nameless peasant girl, haunts him forever."],
["How many copies did it sell, and who starred in the film?","It has sold over 50 million copies; Jean-Jacques Annaud's 1986 film starred Sean Connery as William and a young Christian Slater as Adso."]
]});
QA({"galileo-dialogue":[
["What are the 'two chief world systems'?","The Ptolemaic system, with the Earth motionless at the centre of the universe, and the Copernican system, with the Earth and planets orbiting the Sun; Tycho Brahe's hybrid model is left out."],
["Who are the three speakers?","Salviati argues for Copernicus, Sagredo is an intelligent neutral host, and Simplicio defends Aristotle; the first two are named after Galileo's dead friends Filippo Salviati and Giovanfrancesco Sagredo."],
["Why was the name Simplicio so offensive?","Galileo said it honoured the ancient commentator Simplicius, but it sounds like 'simpleton', and Pope Urban VIII's own favourite argument was put in Simplicio's mouth at the end."],
["What was Urban VIII's argument?","That God in his omnipotence could produce the tides or any effect by means unknown to us, so no physical theory can claim certainty; Galileo placed it as Simplicio's closing remark."],
["What was Galileo's main proof that the Earth moves?","He argued the tides are caused by the Earth's combined daily rotation and yearly orbit sloshing the seas, an argument that was wrong; tides are caused by the Moon and Sun."],
["Why does a stone dropped from a ship's mast land at its foot?","Salviati uses this to show that objects share the motion of the moving Earth, an early statement of the principle of relativity, so we cannot feel Earth's movement."],
["When was it published, and what happened next?","It was printed in Florence in February 1632 with church approval, but in 1633 the Roman Inquisition tried Galileo, found him 'vehemently suspect of heresy', and banned the book."],
["Did Galileo say 'Eppur si muove'?","There is no evidence he muttered 'And yet it moves' at his trial; the phrase first appears in print in Giuseppe Baretti's book of 1757, though a Spanish painting from about 1640 may carry it."],
["What happened to Galileo after the trial?","He was forced to recant on his knees and spent the rest of his life under house arrest at his villa in Arcetri near Florence, where he died, blind, in 1642."],
["When did the Church admit it was wrong?","The Dialogue was removed from the Index of Forbidden Books in 1835, and in 1992 Pope John Paul II formally acknowledged that the Church had erred in condemning Galileo."],
["Why was it written in Italian?","Galileo chose the vernacular rather than Latin so educated laymen could read it, and its witty dialogue form made it a literary as well as scientific classic."]
],
"servant-two-masters":[
["What is The Servant of Two Masters about?","Truffaldino, a hungry servant in Venice, takes a job with two masters at once, Florindo and 'Federigo', who is really Florindo's lover Beatrice in disguise, and juggles them to get two meals."],
["Why is Beatrice dressed as a man?","Her brother Federigo has been killed in a duel by her lover Florindo, who fled; she travels to Venice disguised as Federigo to collect money from his fiancée's father and find Florindo."],
["Who is Truffaldino?","A version of Arlecchino (Harlequin), the comic servant of commedia dell'arte; the role was written for the actor Antonio Sacchi, and later productions call him Arlecchino."],
["What is the dinner scene?","Truffaldino must serve two separate dinners to his two masters at once in the inn, juggling dishes and eating as much as he can, a famous set piece of physical comedy."],
["How does the play end?","Beatrice and Florindo are reunited, Clarice is free to marry her beloved Silvio, and Truffaldino confesses and wins the maid Smeraldina."],
["What is commedia dell'arte?","Italian professional improvised comedy from the 16th century, with stock masked characters such as Arlecchino, Pantalone and Il Dottore, performed from scenarios rather than scripts."],
["What was Goldoni's reform?","Goldoni (1707-1793) replaced improvised masks with written scripts and realistic characters from Venetian life, angering rival Carlo Gozzi, who satirized him in fairy plays."],
["What is One Man, Two Guvnors?","Richard Bean's 2011 adaptation for the National Theatre, set in 1963 Brighton, starring James Corden, who won a Tony for the role on Broadway."],
["What does Pantalone represent?","A miserly old Venetian merchant in red tights, the father of Clarice; his name gave English 'pantaloon' and 'pants'."],
["Where did Goldoni end his life?","In Paris, where he moved in 1762 to direct the Comédie-Italienne, wrote his memoirs in French, and died poor in 1793 after revolutionaries cut his royal pension."]
],
"pinocchio":[
["What does 'Pinocchio' mean?","In Tuscan, 'pinocchio' means pine nut, from 'pino', pine; the carpenter Geppetto names his puppet after a family of beggars he once knew, all called Pinocchio."],
["Where does Collodi's pen name come from?","Carlo Lorenzini (1826-1890) took the name of his mother's hometown, Collodi, a village near Pescia in Tuscany, where there is now a Pinocchio park."],
["When and how was it first published?","It was serialized from 1881 in the Giornale per i bambini in Rome, and published as a book in 1883 with illustrations by Enrico Mazzanti."],
["Did the original Pinocchio die?","Yes: in the first version Pinocchio was hanged from an oak by the Cat and the Fox and the story ended there; readers demanded more, so Collodi revived him with the Blue Fairy."],
["What happens to the talking cricket?","In the original, Pinocchio kills the Talking Cricket with a hammer early on when it lectures him; the cricket later reappears as a ghost."],
["What is the Field of Miracles?","A field near the town of Catchfools where the Cat and the Fox persuade Pinocchio to bury his gold coins, promising they will grow into a money tree, then steal them."],
["What is the Land of Toys?","'Paese dei Balocchi', a paradise where boys play all day without school; after five months Pinocchio and his friend Candlewick grow donkey ears and are sold as donkeys."],
["Where does Pinocchio find Geppetto?","Inside the belly of an enormous shark, the Pesce-cane (in Disney a whale called Monstro), where Geppetto has lived for two years; Pinocchio carries him out on his back."],
["How does the book end?","After working hard to care for Geppetto and the sick Blue Fairy, Pinocchio wakes up as a real boy and sees his old wooden body slumped on a chair."],
["Does Pinocchio's nose always grow when he lies?","It grows only a few times in the book, notably when he lies to the Blue Fairy about his gold coins; Disney's 1940 film made it his defining trait."],
["What adaptations are famous?","Disney's 1940 film, Luigi Comencini's 1972 TV series, Roberto Benigni's 2002 film, Matteo Garrone's 2019 film and Guillermo del Toro's 2022 Oscar-winning stop-motion version."]
],
"house-medlar":[
["What is the title I Malavoglia about?","'The Malavoglia' is the nickname of the Toscano family of fishermen in Aci Trezza near Catania; it means 'ill will' or 'lazy', ironic because they are hardworking."],
["What is the Provvidenza?","The family's fishing boat, 'Providence', which sinks in a storm carrying a cargo of lupins bought on credit, drowning Bastianazzo, the father, and plunging the family into debt."],
["What are lupins, and why do they ruin the family?","Lupini are bitter legume beans eaten as snacks; Padron 'Ntoni buys a load on credit from the usurer Zio Crocifisso, and after the wreck the debt costs them the house by the medlar tree."],
["What happens to young 'Ntoni?","After military service he refuses the hard life, turns to smuggling, stabs the customs guard Don Michele, and goes to prison; on release he leaves the village forever."],
["How does the novel end?","Padron 'Ntoni dies in a hospital, the youngest grandson Alessi buys back the house by the medlar tree, and 'Ntoni says farewell at dawn, knowing he can no longer belong."],
["What is 'verismo'?","An Italian literary movement of the 1870s-90s, inspired by French Naturalism, that portrayed the lives of the poor objectively; Verga and Luigi Capuana led it."],
["What is the 'Cycle of the Vanquished'?","Verga planned five novels on people defeated by the struggle for progress, from fishermen to aristocrats, but completed only I Malavoglia (1881) and Mastro-don Gesualdo (1889)."],
["Why do the characters speak in proverbs?","Verga filled the narration with Sicilian proverbs and a choral village voice to make the narrator disappear, a technique called 'impersonality'."],
["What is the connection to Cavalleria rusticana?","Verga's story Cavalleria rusticana became Mascagni's 1890 opera; Verga sued over royalties and won."],
["Was it filmed?","Luchino Visconti's La terra trema (1948) loosely adapted it, using real fishermen of Aci Trezza speaking Sicilian dialect."]
]});
QA({"il-piacere":[
["Who is Andrea Sperelli?","A young Roman aristocrat, poet and aesthete, D'Annunzio's alter ego, whose rule is to make his life a work of art; he lives in Palazzo Zuccari at the top of the Spanish Steps."],
["Who are the two women in Pleasure?","Elena Muti, a sensual duchess who left Andrea, and Maria Ferres, the pure, musical wife of a Guatemalan diplomat, whom he courts while recovering by the sea."],
["What happens at the duel?","Andrea fights a duel with a rival, Giannetto Rutolo, over an insult and is badly wounded in the chest, then convalesces at his cousin's villa Schifanoja."],
["How does the novel end?","In bed with Maria, Andrea calls out Elena's name and Maria flees; her husband, disgraced for cheating at cards, has his goods auctioned, and Andrea ends following a wardrobe he bought up the stairs."],
["When was it published, and why did it matter?","It appeared in 1889, five years after Huysmans's decadent bible À rebours, and became Italy's key decadent novel, with Andrea as an Italian Dorian Gray before Wilde."],
["What is 'dannunzianesimo'?","The Italian word for D'Annunzio's cult of beauty, luxury, sensual language and heroic posturing, imitated by a generation of writers and dandies."],
["What did D'Annunzio do at Fiume?","In September 1919 he led about 2,600 legionnaires to seize the port of Fiume (now Rijeka, Croatia), ruling it as a state until Italian forces drove him out at 'Bloody Christmas' 1920."],
["Did D'Annunzio fly over Vienna?","Yes: on 9 August 1918 he led a squadron of planes from Italy to Vienna and dropped hundreds of thousands of propaganda leaflets, a famous stunt of the First World War."],
["What is the Vittoriale?","His villa at Gardone Riviera on Lake Garda, crammed with objects, a warship's prow in the garden and his mausoleum, where he lived from 1921 until his death in 1938."],
["What was his relationship with Mussolini?","He was a hero and rival to the Fascists, who borrowed his balcony speeches, salutes and the cry 'Eia, eia, alalà'; Mussolini kept him quiet with honours and money."],
["Who was Eleonora Duse to D'Annunzio?","The great actress was his lover from about 1895; she starred in his plays and financed him, but he betrayed her privacy in his novel Il fuoco (1900)."]
],
"conformist":[
["Who is Marcello Clerici?","A Roman bourgeois haunted by a childhood trauma, who craves normality and becomes a Fascist secret police agent and marries the conventional Giulia to blend in."],
["What was the childhood trauma?","At thirteen, Marcello shot Lino, a chauffeur who had tried to seduce him, and believed he had killed him; this guilt drives his search for conformity."],
["What is Marcello's mission in Paris?","On his honeymoon in 1937 he is to help the secret police assassinate his former professor Edoardo Quadri, an anti-Fascist exile, whom he visits on a pretext."],
["Who is Quadri based on?","The murder echoes the 1937 killing of the anti-Fascist brothers Carlo and Nello Rosselli in France by Cagoule killers acting for Mussolini's regime."],
["Who is Lina?","Quadri's young wife, with whom Marcello falls in love and who is attracted to Giulia; she insists on travelling with her husband and is killed with him."],
["What does Marcello find out at the end?","On the night Mussolini falls in July 1943, he finds Lino alive in Rome, realizing his whole life of conformity has rested on a crime he never committed."],
["How does the novel end?","Fleeing Rome with Giulia and their daughter, Marcello is killed when an Allied plane strafes the road, a different ending from the film."],
["Who made the film, and why is it famous?","Bernardo Bertolucci's 1970 Il conformista, starring Jean-Louis Trintignant, is admired for Vittorio Storaro's cinematography and influenced Coppola and Scorsese."],
["Why was Moravia in trouble with the Fascists?","His novel Gli indifferenti (1929) mocked the bourgeoisie, his father was Jewish, and his cousins were the Rosselli brothers; his books were censored and he hid in Ciociaria in 1943-44."],
["What is Moravia's real name?","Alberto Pincherle (1907-1990); Moravia was his paternal grandmother's surname."]
],
"finzi-continis":[
["Who are the Finzi-Continis?","A wealthy, aloof Jewish family of Ferrara who live in a walled estate, the Barchetto del Duca; the narrator falls for their daughter Micòl."],
["Why do young Jews end up playing tennis in their garden?","After the 1938 Racial Laws, Jews were expelled from the city tennis club, so Micòl and her brother Alberto open their private court to friends."],
["Does Micòl return the narrator's love?","No: she lets him kiss her once but rejects him, telling him they are too alike, and it is later hinted she is involved with Alberto's Communist friend Giampiero Malnate."],
["What happens to Alberto?","Micòl's brother dies of lymphatic cancer in 1942, before the deportations, the only family member to be buried in the family tomb."],
["How does the novel end?","In 1943 the Finzi-Continis are deported; the prologue reveals none came back, and the narrator recalls Micòl as he last saw her."],
["What is the prologue's frame?","In 1957 the narrator visits Etruscan tombs at Cerveteri, and a girl's question about ancient dead prompts him to remember the Finzi-Contini tomb in Ferrara."],
["What were the 1938 Racial Laws?","Laws passed under Mussolini from September 1938 that excluded Jews from schools, universities, the army, public jobs and mixed marriages; about 7,500 Italian Jews were later killed in the Holocaust."],
["Was Bassani's family Jewish?","Yes, Giorgio Bassani (1916-2000) grew up in Ferrara's Jewish community, was jailed in 1943 for anti-Fascist activity, and later as an editor got The Leopard published."],
["Who made the film?","Vittorio De Sica's 1970 film, with Dominique Sanda as Micòl, won the Oscar for Best Foreign Language Film and the Golden Bear."]
],
"tartar-steppe":[
["What is the Tartar Steppe about?","The young officer Giovanni Drogo is posted to Fort Bastiani on the edge of a desert, where the garrison waits for an enemy attack from the north that never comes."],
["Who are the Tartars?","The mythical northern enemies across the desert, of whom there is no trace; the officers' hope of an attack gives meaning to their lives."],
["What happens to Lazzari?","A soldier returns from the desert after catching a stray horse, but doesn't know the password and is shot dead by a sentry who recognizes him but obeys regulations, a death the fort treats as correct procedure."],
["How does the novel end?","After thirty years, when an enemy army finally appears, the sick Major Drogo is sent away to make room for reinforcements and dies alone in an inn, smiling, facing death bravely."],
["Is Fort Bastiani a real place?","No; Buzzati imagined it, though he drew on his years at the Corriere della Sera, where he felt life passing in routine, and on the Dolomites."],
["What does the book say about time?","Drogo believes his real life is still to come, but the 'flight of time' slips away unnoticed, and he wastes his years waiting."],
["When was it published?","In 1940, the year Italy entered the Second World War, by Rizzoli, after the original title La fortezza was changed to avoid war associations."],
["Who was Dino Buzzati?","A journalist, painter and writer from Belluno (1906-1972), who worked for the Corriere della Sera for decades and wrote the children's book The Bears' Famous Invasion of Sicily."],
["Was it filmed?","Valerio Zurlini's Il deserto dei Tartari (1976), filmed in the Arg-e Bam citadel in Iran, starred Jacques Perrin, Vittorio Gassman and Max von Sydow."],
["Why do readers compare it to Kafka?","Its absurd, timeless bureaucracy and endless waiting recall The Castle, and Buzzati was dogged all his life by the label of 'the Italian Kafka', which he resented."]
],
"kaputt":[
["What does 'kaputt' mean?","German for broken, ruined or finished, from French 'capot', losing all tricks at cards; Malaparte uses it for Europe destroyed by the war."],
["Who was Curzio Malaparte?","The pen name of Kurt Erich Suckert (1898-1957), son of a German father; 'Malaparte' (bad side) puns on Bonaparte (good side)."],
["Where was Malaparte during the war?","He was a war correspondent for the Corriere della Sera on the Eastern Front, in Ukraine, Poland and Finland, which gave him material for Kaputt."],
["What is the horse scene?","Hundreds of horses frozen solid in Lake Ladoga, their heads sticking out of the ice, are described in the chapter on Finland, one of the most famous images in war literature."],
["What is the dinner with Hans Frank?","Malaparte describes dining with the Nazi governor of Poland, Hans Frank, in Kraków, while Frank boasts of his rule, intercut with scenes of the ghetto."],
["What is the 'basket of eyes'?","He claims the Croatian dictator Ante Pavelić showed him a basket of what looked like oysters, saying they were 40 pounds of human eyes from Serbs, a scene of disputed truth."],
["Is Kaputt fiction or fact?","It is presented as reportage but heavily embellished; critics dispute many episodes, though it captures the war's horrors."],
["How was the manuscript saved?","Malaparte wrote it secretly in Ukraine and Poland and hid it with friends, smuggling chapters out; it was published in Naples in 1944."],
["What was Malaparte's politics?","He was an early Fascist who took part in the 1922 March on Rome, was exiled to Lipari in 1933 for criticizing Italo Balbo, later flirted with Communism and on his deathbed converted to Catholicism."],
["What is Casa Malaparte?","The red villa he built on a cliff on Capri from 1937, seen in Godard's film Contempt (1963) with Brigitte Bardot."]
],
"brilliant-friend":[
["Who are Lila and Lenù?","Raffaella 'Lila' Cerullo and Elena 'Lenù' Greco, friends from a poor Naples neighbourhood in the 1950s; Lila is brilliant and fierce, Elena studious and the narrator."],
["Who is the 'brilliant friend'?","Elena means Lila, but at the end Lila tells Elena, 'You're my brilliant friend', and that she must keep studying."],
["What starts the novel?","In 2010 Elena hears from Lila's son Rino that Lila, now 66, has vanished without trace, cutting herself out of every photo, so Elena starts writing their story."],
["Who is Don Achille?","A feared loan shark of the neighbourhood, whom Lila and Elena confront as girls; he is stabbed to death in his home, and the Communist carpenter Alfredo Peluso is convicted of the murder."],
["What happens to the dolls?","Lila throws Elena's doll Tina into a cellar, and Elena throws Lila's Nu after it; they go to Don Achille to get them back, and he gives them money instead."],
["How does the first novel end?","At her wedding to the grocer Stefano Carracci at 16, Lila sees Marcello Solara, whom she hates, wearing the shoes she designed, which Stefano had sold to the Solaras."],
["Who are the Solara brothers?","Marcello and Michele, sons of a Camorra-linked café owner, who dominate the neighbourhood with money and violence."],
["Who is Elena Ferrante?","A pseudonymous author; in 2016 the journalist Claudio Gatti claimed she is translator Anita Raja, but the author has never confirmed her identity."],
["How many novels are there?","Four Neapolitan Novels: My Brilliant Friend (2011), The Story of a New Name, Those Who Leave and Those Who Stay, and The Story of the Lost Child (2014)."],
["Is there a TV adaptation?","HBO and RAI's L'amica geniale (2018-2024), directed by Saverio Costanzo and others, filmed in Neapolitan dialect with subtitles in Italy."],
["What is 'smarginatura'?","'Dissolving margins', Lila's term for moments when the edges of people and things seem to dissolve, terrifying her, as on New Year's Eve 1958."]
]});
QA({"vita-nuova":[
["What does 'Vita Nuova' mean?","'New Life', meaning the life renewed by love for Beatrice, or possibly just 'youth'; Dante wrote it around 1293-95, after Beatrice's death in 1290."],
["How is the Vita Nuova structured?","It is a prosimetrum: 31 poems, mostly sonnets plus canzoni and a ballata, framed by prose that tells the story behind each and analyses its parts."],
["How old were Dante and Beatrice when they met?","Both were nine; he saw her dressed in crimson, met her again nine years later when she greeted him in the street, and the number nine recurs throughout."],
["Why is the number nine so important to Dante?","Nine is the square of three, the Trinity, so Dante says Beatrice herself was 'a nine', a miracle whose root is the Trinity."],
["What is a 'screen lady'?","A 'donna dello schermo', a woman Dante pretended to love to hide his real love for Beatrice; gossip about it made Beatrice refuse to greet him."],
["What is the dream of the burning heart?","In the first sonnet Love appears holding the sleeping Beatrice and Dante's burning heart, which he makes her eat before weeping and rising to heaven with her."],
["What is 'Tanto gentile e tanto onesta pare'?","The most famous sonnet of the book, 'So gentle and so honest seems my lady', describing Beatrice's greeting that makes every tongue tremble and fall silent."],
["Who was the 'first friend' Dante dedicated it to?","Guido Cavalcanti, the poet of the dolce stil novo, whom Dante called his first friend; Dante later had to approve his exile in 1300."],
["What is the dolce stil novo?","The 'sweet new style', Dante's term in Purgatorio 24 for the Tuscan love poetry of Guinizelli, Cavalcanti and himself, which idealized the beloved as an angelic messenger."],
["How does the Vita Nuova end?","After a 'marvellous vision', Dante resolves to write no more of Beatrice until he can say of her 'what has never been said of any woman', a promise kept in the Divine Comedy."],
["Who is the 'donna gentile'?","A compassionate lady at a window who consoles Dante after Beatrice's death, tempting him to forget her; he repents, and in the later Convivio he says she was Philosophy."]
],
"mandragola":[
["What is a mandrake, and why does it matter in the play?","The mandrake, mandragora, is a plant with a forked, human-shaped root once believed to cure infertility; the trickster Callimaco claims a potion of it will make Lucrezia pregnant."],
["What is the plot of The Mandrake?","Callimaco, in love with Lucrezia, wife of the elderly lawyer Nicia, poses as a doctor and convinces Nicia the mandrake potion will give him an heir but kill the first man to sleep with her."],
["How does the trick get Callimaco into Lucrezia's bed?","Nicia agrees to have a stranger kidnapped to sleep with Lucrezia and absorb the poison; the 'stranger' grabbed in the street is Callimaco himself in disguise."],
["How is Lucrezia persuaded?","Her mother Sostrata and her corrupt confessor Fra Timoteo, bribed with money, convince her that the sin is excused by the good of having a child."],
["How does the play end?","Lucrezia, learning the truth, chooses to keep Callimaco as her lover permanently, and the grateful Nicia gives him a key to the house and invites him to church."],
["Who is Ligurio?","A parasite and matchmaker who masterminds the scheme, an embodiment of Machiavellian cunning in comedy."],
["When was the Mandrake written?","Around 1518, during Machiavelli's years of exile from politics, and it was first performed in Florence, soon becoming the most popular comedy of its day."],
["Why is Lucrezia's name significant?","It recalls the Roman Lucretia, raped by Tarquin's son, who killed herself and inspired the overthrow of the monarchy; Machiavelli's Lucrezia instead accepts the adultery."],
["Who saw The Mandrake performed?","It was a hit, staged for Pope Leo X in Rome and widely in Venice, and is often called the finest Italian comedy of the Renaissance."],
["What does it have in common with The Prince?","Like his treatise, it rewards those who use cunning and calculate ends, with Ligurio and Fra Timoteo applying political realism to sex and religion."]
],
"jerusalem-delivered":[
["What is Jerusalem Delivered about?","Tasso's epic tells the climax of the First Crusade in 1099, when Godfrey of Bouillon's crusaders besiege and capture Jerusalem."],
["Who are Rinaldo and Armida?","The crusader hero Rinaldo is enchanted by the sorceress Armida in her garden on a distant island, until two knights rescue him with a magic shield; she later offers herself to him as his handmaid."],
["Who are Tancredi and Clorinda?","The Christian knight Tancredi loves the Muslim warrior-woman Clorinda, kills her in armour at night in a duel without recognizing her, and baptizes her as she dies."],
["What is the enchanted forest?","The magician Ismeno bewitches the forest whose wood the crusaders need for siege towers, so monsters and illusions frighten away cutters until Rinaldo breaks the spell."],
["Who is Erminia?","A Muslim princess who loves Tancredi, flees disguised in Clorinda's armour, shelters among shepherds, and finally nurses the wounded Tancredi."],
["How does it end?","The crusaders storm Jerusalem, Rinaldo kills Solimano, and Godfrey hangs up his arms and worships at the Holy Sepulchre."],
["Why did Tasso go mad?","Tasso suffered paranoia and fear of the Inquisition; Duke Alfonso II d'Este had him confined in the hospital of Sant'Anna in Ferrara from 1579 to 1586."],
["When was it published?","Pirated editions appeared in 1580 and 1581 without Tasso's consent; he later rewrote it as Gerusalemme conquistata (1593), which few readers prefer."],
["What music came from it?","Monteverdi's Combattimento di Tancredi e Clorinda, Handel's Rinaldo, Lully's and Gluck's Armide, and Rossini's Armida."],
["Did Goethe write about Tasso?","Yes: his play Torquato Tasso (1790) dramatizes the poet's conflicts at the Ferrara court, and Byron wrote The Lament of Tasso."]
],
"polo-travels":[
["What is the Travels' original title?","It is often called Il Milione, possibly from the Polo family nickname Emilione, or from Marco's habit of speaking in millions; its French title was Le Divisament dou monde."],
["How was the book written?","In 1298 Marco Polo, a prisoner of war in Genoa after a sea battle, dictated his stories to fellow prisoner Rustichello da Pisa, a writer of Arthurian romances, who wrote them in French."],
["How long was Marco Polo away from Venice?","About 24 years: he left with his father Niccolò and uncle Maffeo in 1271, aged 17, and returned in 1295."],
["Did Marco Polo work for Kublai Khan?","He says he served the Mongol emperor of China for about 17 years, as an envoy and even as governor of Yangzhou, a claim historians doubt."],
["How did the Polos get home?","They escorted a Mongol princess, Kököchin, by sea from China to Persia to marry the Ilkhan, sailing through Sumatra and India, arriving around 1293."],
["What is Xanadu?","Shangdu, Kublai's summer capital in Inner Mongolia, which Polo described with its marble palace and bamboo pavilion; Coleridge's Kubla Khan was inspired by a version of the account."],
["What surprising things did Polo describe?","Paper money, coal as 'black stones that burn', the postal relay system, and the vast city of Quinsai (Hangzhou), which he says had 12,000 bridges."],
["Did Marco Polo really go to China?","Most historians think he did, citing accurate details on salt production and paper money, but skeptics note he never mentions the Great Wall, tea or foot-binding."],
["Did Marco Polo bring pasta from China?","No; that is a myth spread in the 1920s by an American pasta trade magazine, and dried pasta was known in Italy before his return."],
["What did Columbus have to do with Marco Polo?","Columbus owned and annotated a Latin edition of the Travels and hoped to reach the lands of the Great Khan by sailing west."],
["What did Polo say on his deathbed?","According to a later chronicler, when friends urged him to retract his tales, he replied 'I have not told half of what I saw'."]
],
"little-flowers":[
["What does 'Fioretti' mean?","'Little flowers', a medieval term for an anthology of choice stories; the Fioretti di San Francesco is a late 14th-century Italian version of the Latin Actus beati Francisci."],
["What is the story of the wolf of Gubbio?","St Francis tames a man-eating wolf by making a pact: the townspeople feed it, it stops attacking, and it lives peacefully in Gubbio for two years until it dies."],
["What is the sermon to the birds?","Near Bevagna, Francis preaches to a flock of birds, telling them to praise God for their feathers and food, and they stay still until he blesses them."],
["What is 'perfect joy'?","Francis tells Brother Leo that perfect joy is not miracles or converts but being turned away at night, cold and soaked, from their own friary and accepting it patiently."],
["What are the stigmata?","The wounds of Christ's crucifixion, which Francis is said to have received on Mount La Verna in September 1224, appended to the Fioretti as the 'Considerations on the Stigmata'."],
["Who are Brother Juniper and Brother Leo?","Juniper is the holy fool who cuts off a pig's foot to feed a sick brother; Leo is Francis's companion and confessor."],
["Who was St Francis?","Francesco di Bernardone (c. 1181-1226), son of a rich cloth merchant of Assisi, who renounced wealth, founded the Franciscan order in 1209 and was canonized in 1228."],
["Why does Francis call poverty a lady?","He imagined 'Lady Poverty' as his bride, a chivalric image of his vow to own nothing, as Dante describes in Paradiso 11."],
["Who was St Clare?","Clare of Assisi, a noblewoman who followed Francis and founded the Poor Clares; in one Fioretti story she and Francis share a meal that lights up the woods."],
["What is the Canticle of the Sun?","Francis's own vernacular hymn praising God for 'Brother Sun' and 'Sister Moon', one of the first poems in Italian, written around 1224-25."],
["Who chose the name Francis to honour him?","Pope Francis, elected in 2013, the first pope to take the name, explained he chose it in honour of Francis of Assisi and his love of the poor."]
]});
QA({"new-science":[
["What is The New Science about?","Giambattista Vico's Scienza Nuova (1725, revised 1744) proposes a science of human history, showing nations pass through recurring stages shaped by language, myth and institutions."],
["What are Vico's three ages?","The age of gods (theocratic, ruled by fear and oracles), the age of heroes (aristocratic), and the age of men (democratic), followed by decline and a return, the 'ricorso'."],
["What is the 'verum factum' principle?","Vico's idea that we can truly know only what we have made, so humans can understand history and society better than nature, which God made."],
["What are 'corsi e ricorsi'?","'Courses and recourses', Vico's notion that history cycles through ages, then falls into a new barbarism and begins again."],
["What did Vico say about Homer?","He argued that 'Homer' was not one poet but the Greek people themselves, whose collective memory produced the epics over centuries, anticipating later scholarship."],
["Who was Vico?","A Neapolitan professor of rhetoric (1668-1744), poorly paid and largely ignored during his life, who failed to win the chair of law he wanted."],
["What is 'poetic wisdom'?","Vico's idea that early humans thought in images, myths and metaphors rather than abstractions, so myths encode real history."],
["How did Vico influence James Joyce?","Finnegans Wake is built on Vico's cycles; its first word 'riverrun' and circular structure, ending mid-sentence to loop to the start, echo the ricorso."],
["What is the frontispiece of the New Science?","An allegorical engraving showing Metaphysics on a globe, lit by divine providence, with Homer's statue and symbols of human institutions, which Vico explains at length."],
["Who rediscovered Vico?","Jules Michelet translated him into French in 1827, and Benedetto Croce championed him in the 20th century; Isaiah Berlin wrote about him as a counter-Enlightenment thinker."]
],
"crimes-punishments":[
["When was On Crimes and Punishments published?","In 1764, anonymously, in Livorno, because its attack on torture and the death penalty was dangerous; Cesare Beccaria was 26."],
["What is Beccaria's argument against the death penalty?","He argued the state has no right to take life, that executions are less of a deterrent than lifelong forced labour, and that public killing brutalizes society."],
["What did Beccaria say about torture?","He called it cruel and absurd: it punishes before guilt is proven, makes the strong innocent confess and lets the robust guilty go free."],
["What does 'the greatest happiness of the greatest number' come from?","Beccaria wrote 'la massima felicità divisa nel maggior numero', which Jeremy Bentham credited as inspiration for utilitarianism."],
["What makes punishment effective, according to Beccaria?","Certainty and promptness of punishment deter better than severity, so laws should be clear, public and proportionate."],
["Who were the Verri brothers?","Pietro and Alessandro Verri ran the Milanese Enlightenment circle 'Accademia dei Pugni' and its journal Il Caffè, and pushed Beccaria to write the book."],
["Which ruler abolished capital punishment after reading him?","Grand Duke Leopold of Tuscany abolished the death penalty on 30 November 1786, making Tuscany the first modern state to do so."],
["How was the book received?","It was placed on the Catholic Index in 1766, translated into French by Morellet, praised by Voltaire, and admired by Catherine the Great and Thomas Jefferson."],
["Is Beccaria related to Manzoni?","Yes: his daughter Giulia was the mother of Alessandro Manzoni, author of The Betrothed."],
["Did Beccaria's ideas influence the US Constitution?","Jefferson copied long passages into his commonplace book and John Adams quoted him defending the Boston Massacre soldiers in 1770, so he fed the founders' debates on punishment, though the 'cruel and unusual' wording comes from England's 1689 Bill of Rights."]
]});
QA({"mattia-pascal":[
["What is the premise of The Late Mattia Pascal?","Mattia Pascal, a librarian trapped in a bad marriage in a Ligurian town, runs away, wins a fortune at Monte Carlo, and reads in a newspaper that he has been identified as a drowned body in his mill race."],
["What new identity does Mattia take?","He calls himself Adriano Meis and settles in Rome, but without papers he can't marry, report a theft or fight a duel, so his freedom becomes a kind of nonexistence."],
["How does Mattia escape his second identity?","He fakes Adriano Meis's suicide by leaving his hat and cane on a bridge over the Tiber, and returns home as Mattia Pascal."],
["How does the novel end?","Back home he finds his wife Romilda remarried to his friend Pomino with a child, so he lives in the town as an outsider, laying flowers on his own grave."],
["Who is Adriana?","The gentle daughter of his Roman landlord Anselmo Paleari, whom Adriano Meis loves but cannot marry because legally he does not exist."],
["What is the 'tear in the paper sky'?","Anselmo Paleari imagines a puppet Orestes looking up at a hole in a paper-sky theatre and losing his tragic will, a symbol of modern self-doubt."],
["When was it published?","Serialized in 1904 in the magazine Nuova Antologia, it was Pirandello's first big success, written while his wife was ill after the family's financial ruin."],
["Why does Mattia say Copernicus ruined humanity?","He jokes that by showing the Earth was not the centre of the universe, Copernicus made human affairs seem trivial, so detailed histories no longer matter."],
["Was it filmed?","Marcel L'Herbier's French silent film Feu Mathias Pascal (1926) and Mario Monicelli's Le due vite di Mattia Pascal (1985) with Marcello Mastroianni."],
["What did Mattia's library job involve?","He was librarian of the Boccamazza library, a disused church full of mouldy books, where he and rats were the only visitors."]
],
"montale":[
["What does 'Ossi di seppia' mean?","'Cuttlefish bones', the white skeletons washed up on Ligurian beaches, symbols of the poet's dry, stripped-down life; the collection was published in 1925."],
["Where are the poems set?","On the Ligurian coast at Monterosso al Mare in the Cinque Terre, where Montale spent his childhood summers in a family villa."],
["What is 'Non chiederci la parola'?","'Don't ask us for the word', a poem refusing to offer a formula of truth: the only thing the poet can say is 'what we are not, what we do not want'."],
["What is 'Meriggiare pallido e assorto'?","'To spend the noon pale and absorbed', a poem about a hot afternoon by a wall topped with broken bottle glass, symbol of life's barrier."],
["What is the 'male di vivere'?","'The evil of living', from 'Spesso il male di vivere ho incontrato', which Montale says he encountered in a choked stream, a curled leaf and a fallen horse."],
["What is 'correlativo oggettivo'?","The objective correlative, T.S. Eliot's term, which Italian critics apply to Montale: concrete objects like the cuttlefish bones that express emotions without naming them."],
["Did Montale win the Nobel?","Yes, in 1975, 'for his distinctive poetry which, with great artistic sensitivity, has interpreted human values under the sign of an outlook on life with no illusions'."],
["What did Montale do under Fascism?","He signed Croce's 1925 Manifesto of anti-Fascist intellectuals and in 1938 lost his job as director of Florence's Gabinetto Vieusseux library for refusing to join the party."],
["Who was Montale's muse Clizia?","Irma Brandeis, an American Dante scholar he met in Florence in 1933, who appears as Clizia in his later collection Le occasioni."],
["Was Montale a singer?","He trained as an operatic baritone in his youth and later worked as a music critic for the Corriere della Sera."]
],
"christ-eboli":[
["What does the title mean?","The peasants of Lucania say 'Christ stopped at Eboli', meaning Christianity, civilization and the state never reached beyond that town into their poor hills."],
["Why was Carlo Levi in Lucania?","A Turinese doctor, painter and anti-Fascist, he was sentenced to 'confino' (internal exile) in 1935-36 in Grassano and Aliano, which he calls Gagliano."],
["What is 'confino'?","Fascist internal exile, by which political opponents were sent to remote villages or islands under police watch; Levi was released in 1936 after the conquest of Ethiopia."],
["Why is Eboli significant geographically?","Eboli is a town south of Salerno where the road and railway turn inland away from the coast, marking the edge of the 'civilized' south."],
["What did Levi find in Aliano?","Malaria, extreme poverty, superstition and hostility to the state; peasants hung pictures of the Madonna and Franklin D. Roosevelt side by side."],
["Who is the podestà?","Don Luigi Magalone, the pompous Fascist mayor, who represents petty, self-serving authority and keeps watch over Levi."],
["Why did peasants trust Levi?","He practised medicine for free despite the local doctors' jealousy, and they came to regard him almost as a magician."],
["When was it published?","Written in Florence in 1943-44 while Levi hid from the Germans, it was published by Einaudi in 1945."],
["Who made the film?","Francesco Rosi's 1979 film starred Gian Maria Volonté as Levi."],
["What changed in the South after the book?","It helped draw attention to the 'Southern Question', and the cave dwellings of Matera, the 'Sassi', were evacuated in the 1950s; Matera became European Capital of Culture in 2019."]
],
"if-this-is-a-man":[
["What does the title mean?","It comes from Levi's opening poem, 'Consider if this is a man, who works in the mud... who dies because of a yes or a no'; the US edition was called Survival in Auschwitz."],
["How did Primo Levi end up in Auschwitz?","He joined a partisan group in the Aosta Valley, was captured in December 1943, admitted to being Jewish, and was deported from the Fossoli camp in February 1944."],
["What was Levi's number?","174517, tattooed on his left arm at Monowitz (Auschwitz III), the camp attached to the IG Farben Buna plant."],
["How many of his transport survived?","Of 650 Italian Jews on his train, about 20 came home; 96 men and 29 women were selected for work, and the rest were gassed on arrival."],
["What is the Canto of Ulysses chapter?","Levi tries to recite Dante's Inferno 26 to his friend Jean Samuel, 'Pikolo', and realizes the line 'you were not made to live like brutes' as a revelation."],
["How did Levi survive?","He passed a chemistry exam to work in the lab, his friend Lorenzo Perrone, a civilian worker, gave him soup daily, and he caught scarlet fever just as the SS evacuated the camp."],
["What happened in the last ten days?","The SS left the sick behind in January 1945; Levi and two Frenchmen kept a ward alive with a stove and stolen food until the Red Army arrived on 27 January."],
["What are 'Muselmänner'?","Camp slang for prisoners so starved and broken they had given up, called 'the drowned' by Levi, as opposed to 'the saved'."],
["Why was the book first rejected?","Einaudi, with Natalia Ginzburg as reader, turned it down in 1947; it was printed by the small Turin press De Silva in about 2,500 copies and only republished by Einaudi in 1958."],
["What does 'Wstawać' mean?","Polish for 'get up', the dawn command that woke prisoners in the camp, which Levi says haunted his dreams."],
["What is the sequel?","The Truce (1963) describes his long journey home through Eastern Europe, and The Drowned and the Saved (1986) reflects on the camps."]
],
"baron-trees":[
["Why does Cosimo climb into the trees?","On 15 June 1767, aged twelve, Cosimo Piovasco di Rondò refuses to eat a dish of snails cooked by his sister Battista, climbs an oak and vows never to come down."],
["Does he ever come down?","No: he lives the rest of his life in the trees of Ombrosa, and as an old man grabs the rope of a passing hot-air balloon and disappears out to sea."],
["Who narrates the story?","His younger brother Biagio, who stays on the ground, inherits the title, and tells Cosimo's life with admiration and bewilderment."],
["Who is Viola?","A wilful noble girl from a neighbouring family, Cosimo's great love, who leaves him after jealous quarrels and marries an English lord."],
["What does Cosimo do in the trees?","He reads Enlightenment books, corresponds with Diderot, fights pirates, organizes fire brigades, meets Napoleon, and writes a project for a constitution."],
["What does Cosimo's epitaph say?","'Cosimo Piovasco di Rondò: lived in trees, always loved the earth, went up into the sky'."],
["Is it part of a trilogy?","Yes: Our Ancestors, with The Cloven Viscount (1952) and The Nonexistent Knight (1959); The Baron in the Trees appeared in 1957."],
["What happened to Calvino politically in 1957?","He left the Italian Communist Party after the Soviet invasion of Hungary, and the novel's theme of detached engagement reflects that break."],
["Who is Gian dei Brughi?","A feared brigand whom Cosimo turns into a reader by lending him novels; addicted to books, he is captured and hanged while wanting to know how a novel ends."],
["Where is Ombrosa?","A fictional town on the Ligurian Riviera, like Sanremo, where Calvino grew up; the name means 'shady'."]
],
"pereira":[
["Who is Pereira?","Dr Pereira, an aging, overweight widowed journalist in Lisbon in 1938 who edits the culture page of a small evening paper, Lisboa, and talks to his late wife's portrait."],
["What does 'sostiene Pereira' mean?","'Pereira maintains', a refrain repeated throughout, as if Pereira is giving testimony to an unnamed authority."],
["What was happening in Portugal in 1938?","António Salazar's Estado Novo dictatorship ruled, with censorship and the PVDE secret police, while the Spanish Civil War raged next door."],
["Who is Monteiro Rossi?","A young radical with an Italian father whom Pereira hires to write advance obituaries; his pieces are unprintable, but Pereira helps him and his girlfriend Marta hide."],
["How does the novel end?","Secret police beat Monteiro Rossi to death in Pereira's flat; Pereira publishes an exposé by tricking the censor, then flees Portugal with a forged passport."],
["What is Dr Cardoso's 'confederation of souls'?","A doctor at a thalassotherapy clinic explains to Pereira that each of us is a confederation of souls ruled by a 'hegemonic ego' that can change, freeing him to change."],
["What does Pereira drink?","Endless lemonade with lots of sugar, which Dr Cardoso urges him to give up along with omelettes for the sake of his heart."],
["Why did Tabucchi love Portugal?","Antonio Tabucchi (1943-2012) was a scholar and translator of Fernando Pessoa, lived partly in Lisbon, and died there."],
["Was it filmed?","Roberto Faenza's Sostiene Pereira (1995) starred Marcello Mastroianni in one of his last roles."],
["Why was it a political sensation in Italy?","Published in 1994 when Silvio Berlusconi took power, it was read as a call for intellectual courage, and won the Campiello and Viareggio prizes."]
],
"danube-magris":[
["What is Danube about?","Claudio Magris follows the Danube from its disputed source in Germany to the Black Sea, mixing travelogue with history, literature and biography of Central Europe."],
["Where is the source of the Danube?","Officially at Donaueschingen in the Black Forest, where the Breg and Brigach streams meet, but the source is disputed, and Magris opens the book playfully weighing the rival claims."],
["What is 'Mitteleuropa'?","Central Europe, especially the multinational world of the Habsburg Empire, whose cultural legacy Magris studied in The Habsburg Myth (1963)."],
["When was Danube published?","In 1986 in Italian, and in English in 1989, the year the Berlin Wall fell."],
["Who was Claudio Magris?","A Triestine scholar of German literature, born 1939, a senator in the 1990s, and a perennial Nobel candidate."],
["Who appears in Danube?","Heidegger, Kafka, Céline, Lukács, Elias Canetti and Empress Maria Theresa, among many figures linked to places on the river."],
["Where does the Danube end?","In the Danube Delta in Romania, at the Black Sea, at Sulina, where Magris ends the book."],
["Why is Trieste important to Magris?","Trieste, former Habsburg port, is a crossroads of Italian, Slavic and German cultures, central to his idea of borders and identity."],
["What prize did Danube win?","It won Italy's Bagutta Prize in 1986, and Magris later received the Prince of Asturias Award for Letters in 2004."],
["How long is the Danube?","About 2,850 km, flowing through ten countries, more than any other river in the world."]
]});
QA({"pico-oration":[
["What is the Oration on the Dignity of Man?","A speech Giovanni Pico della Mirandola wrote in 1486 to open a public debate in Rome on his 900 theses, arguing that humans uniquely can shape their own nature."],
["What are the 900 theses?","Propositions drawn from Plato, Aristotle, the Kabbalah, Zoroaster and Hermes Trismegistus, which Pico, aged 23, offered to defend against any scholar at his own expense."],
["Why was the debate cancelled?","Pope Innocent VIII suspended it in 1487 and condemned thirteen theses as heretical; Pico fled to France, was briefly arrested, and later found refuge in Florence under Lorenzo de' Medici."],
["What does God tell Adam in the Oration?","God says he gave Adam no fixed place or form, so he may degenerate into the brutes or be reborn into the divine, according to his own choice."],
["Why is the title misleading?","Pico didn't name it so; the phrase 'on the dignity of man' was added by later editors, and he called it simply an oration."],
["What is the Kabbalah, and why was Pico interested?","Jewish mystical interpretation of scripture; Pico was the first Christian to study it seriously, believing it confirmed Christian truths, founding Christian Kabbalah."],
["How did Pico die?","He died in Florence in November 1494, aged 31, the day Charles VIII entered the city; exhumation in 2007 confirmed arsenic poisoning, possibly by his secretary."],
["Who was Pico's friend Savonarola?","The fiery Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola, whom Pico persuaded Lorenzo to bring to Florence, and who preached at Pico's funeral."],
["What was Pico famous for besides the Oration?","His prodigious memory and learning in Greek, Hebrew, Aramaic and Arabic, which made him the model Renaissance prodigy."],
["What does 'chameleon' have to do with Pico?","He called man 'this chameleon of ours', able to change into any form, the Oration's most famous image."]
],
"alberti-painting":[
["What is On Painting about?","Leon Battista Alberti's treatise, written in Latin (De pictura, 1435) and Italian (Della pittura, 1436), explains linear perspective, composition and the purpose of painting."],
["Who was Alberti?","A humanist, architect and writer (1404-1472), illegitimate son of an exiled Florentine family, who designed Santa Maria Novella's façade and the Tempio Malatestiano in Rimini."],
["What is the 'open window' idea?","Alberti said a painting should be like an open window through which we see the scene, a definition of Renaissance pictorial space."],
["Who did Alberti dedicate the Italian version to?","Filippo Brunelleschi, architect of Florence's cathedral dome, who had demonstrated perspective experimentally, along with Donatello, Ghiberti, Masaccio and Luca della Robbia."],
["What is the 'istoria'?","The narrative subject of a painting, which Alberti said was the painter's greatest work, combining varied figures, gestures and emotions."],
["What is the 'velo'?","A veil of thin cloth with a grid of threads, stretched between eye and subject, which Alberti invented to help painters transfer what they saw accurately."],
["What did Alberti say about the vanishing point?","He described the 'centric point' where parallel lines meet on the horizon, at the height of the viewer's eye, the basis of single-point perspective."],
["Why did he write it in two languages?","The Latin version addressed humanist scholars, the Italian one practising artists who did not read Latin."],
["What are Alberti's other famous books?","De re aedificatoria, on architecture, modelled on Vitruvius, and Della famiglia, dialogues on family life; he also invented the cipher wheel."],
["Was Alberti a 'Renaissance man'?","Yes: Jacob Burckhardt held him up as the universal man, praising tales of his athletic feats, like jumping over a man standing still."]
],
"leonardo-notebooks":[
["How many pages of Leonardo's notebooks survive?","About 7,000 pages, perhaps half of what he wrote, scattered among collections like the Codex Atlanticus in Milan and the Royal Collection at Windsor."],
["Why did Leonardo write backwards?","He wrote in mirror script, right to left, probably because he was left-handed and it avoided smudging, rather than to keep secrets."],
["What is the Codex Leicester?","A notebook on water, rocks and fossils written around 1508-10, bought by Bill Gates in 1994 for about 30.8 million dollars, the most expensive book ever sold at the time."],
["What is the Vitruvian Man?","Leonardo's drawing of around 1490 of a man inscribed in a circle and square, illustrating the Roman architect Vitruvius's ideal proportions; it is kept in Venice's Accademia."],
["Did Leonardo design a helicopter?","He sketched an 'aerial screw' of linen and wood, and designs for flying machines, a parachute, an armoured vehicle and a diving suit."],
["What anatomical discoveries did Leonardo make?","He dissected about 30 corpses, drew the first accurate fetus in the womb, and described the heart's valves and blood flow, but never published his findings."],
["Who inherited the notebooks?","His pupil Francesco Melzi, who compiled the Treatise on Painting; after Melzi's death in 1570, his heirs dispersed the papers."],
["What are the notebooks' everyday contents?","Shopping lists, debts, riddles, jokes, fables, and to-do lists like 'describe the tongue of the woodpecker'."],
["Why did Leonardo call himself 'omo sanza lettere'?","He called himself a man without letters, meaning without Latin learning, and insisted experience was the true teacher."],
["What is the Codex Atlanticus?","The largest collection, about 1,100 sheets, bound by the sculptor Pompeo Leoni in the late 16th century, now in the Biblioteca Ambrosiana in Milan."]
],
"guicciardini-ricordi":[
["What are the Ricordi?","Francesco Guicciardini's collection of about 220 maxims and reflections on politics and human nature, written and revised between 1512 and 1530 and published after his death."],
["Who was Guicciardini?","A Florentine lawyer, diplomat and papal governor (1483-1540), friend and critic of Machiavelli, and author of the History of Italy."],
["How did Guicciardini disagree with Machiavelli?","He rejected Machiavelli's habit of drawing rules from ancient Rome, saying each situation must be judged with 'discrezione', practical discernment."],
["What is 'il particulare'?","One's own private interest, which Guicciardini said guides people and which the wise man should look after, a phrase critics later saw as Italian selfishness."],
["What was his job under the popes?","He governed Modena, Reggio, Parma and the Romagna for the Medici popes Leo X and Clement VII, and was lieutenant general of the papal army."],
["What is the History of Italy?","His account of Italian politics from 1490 to 1534, including the French invasion and Sack of Rome, often called the first modern historical work."],
["What did he say about priests?","He confessed that his work for popes forced him to love their greatness for his own sake, though he would have loved Luther as much, to see the priests reduced to their proper place."],
["What happened to Guicciardini at the end?","He helped Cosimo I de' Medici become duke in 1537, but was sidelined and retired to his villa at Arcetri, dying in 1540."],
["Was Guicciardini Machiavelli's friend?","Yes: when Machiavelli was sent in 1521 to a Franciscan chapter at Carpi, near Guicciardini's Modena, the two swapped comic letters and staged messengers to impress the friars."]
],
"discourses-livy":[
["What are the Discourses on Livy?","Machiavelli's commentary on the first ten books of Livy's history of Rome, written around 1513-17, analysing why the Roman Republic grew great and free."],
["How do the Discourses differ from The Prince?","They praise republics as more stable, prudent and grateful than princes, arguing that the people are better guardians of liberty than nobles."],
["What does Machiavelli say about conflict between nobles and plebs?","He argues the tumults between Rome's patricians and plebeians were not a weakness but the cause of its freedom and laws."],
["What is a 'return to beginnings'?","Machiavelli's idea that republics and religions must periodically go back to their founding principles, through laws or great men, to avoid corruption."],
["What does Machiavelli say about religion?","He praises Roman religion for inspiring soldiers and blames the Church for keeping Italy divided and making people weak."],
["Who were the Discourses dedicated to?","To his friends Zanobi Buondelmonti and Cosimo Rucellai, members of the Orti Oricellari circle in Florence."],
["What does Machiavelli say about founders?","A founder must be alone, and may use violence, as Romulus killed his brother Remus, if the result is good for the community."],
["When were they published?","In 1531, four years after his death, a year before The Prince."],
["Why are the Discourses important for modern politics?","They inspired republican thinkers like Harrington, Montesquieu and the American founders with ideas about mixed government and checks on power."],
["What did Machiavelli say about conspiracies?","Book 3, chapter 6, the longest, argues conspiracies usually fail, because many people must be trusted and nerve fails at the moment of action."]
]});
QA({"ungaretti-allegria":[
["What does 'L'Allegria' mean?","'Joy' or 'gaiety', an ironic title for poems written in the trenches of the First World War; the collection grew from Il porto sepolto (1916) to L'Allegria (1931)."],
["What is 'Mattina'?","A two-line poem, 'M'illumino / d'immenso', 'I illuminate myself with immensity', written at Santa Maria la Longa in January 1917, one of the shortest famous poems in any language."],
["What is 'Soldati'?","A four-line poem comparing soldiers to leaves on trees in autumn, 'Si sta come / d'autunno / sugli alberi / le foglie', written in the Bosco di Courton in 1918."],
["Where did Ungaretti fight?","As a private in the 19th Infantry Regiment on the Carso plateau near the Isonzo, where many poems are dated by place and day."],
["What is 'Veglia'?","'Vigil', a poem about spending a night beside a slaughtered comrade, ending with the poet writing letters full of love and never feeling so attached to life."],
["What is 'Il porto sepolto'?","'The buried port', the title of his first collection, refers to an ancient submerged harbour at Alexandria, a symbol of hidden poetic truth."],
["Where was Ungaretti born?","In Alexandria, Egypt, in 1888, to Italian parents from Lucca; he studied in Paris and knew Apollinaire before the war."],
["What is 'Ermetismo'?","Hermeticism, a style of compressed, obscure poetry of the 1920s-30s associated with Ungaretti, Montale and Quasimodo, using pared-down words and white space."],
["What is 'I fiumi'?","'The rivers', a poem in which the poet, bathing in the Isonzo, recalls the Serchio of his ancestors, the Nile of his birth and the Seine of his studies."],
["Was Ungaretti a Fascist?","He signed the 1925 Manifesto of Fascist Intellectuals, and Mussolini wrote the preface to the 1923 edition of Il porto sepolto."]
],
"saba-canzoniere":[
["Who was Umberto Saba?","The pen name of Umberto Poli (1883-1957), a Triestine poet and antiquarian bookseller, son of a Jewish mother abandoned by his father."],
["Why did he choose the name Saba?","Possibly in honour of his wet nurse Peppa Sabaz, a Slovene woman who cared for him in early childhood, or from Hebrew 'saba', grandfather."],
["What is the Canzoniere?","Saba's life's work, a single collection of all his poems, first published in 1921 and expanded through 1961, telling his life like a novel in verse."],
["What is 'A mia moglie'?","'To my wife', a poem comparing his wife Lina to a white pullet, a heifer, a dog, a rabbit, a swallow and an ant, which first shocked readers."],
["What is 'Trieste'?","A poem describing the city's 'scontrosa grazia', surly grace, like a rough boy with big hands, and a hidden corner for the poet's solitary life."],
["What is 'Storia e cronistoria del Canzoniere'?","A book in which Saba commented on his own poems under the pseudonym Giuseppe Carimandrei, published in 1948."],
["How did the Racial Laws affect Saba?","After 1938 he had to put his bookshop in his assistant Carlo Cerne's name, fled to Paris, then hid in Florence during the German occupation, helped by friends including Montale."],
["Was Saba psychoanalysed?","Yes: in 1929 he began analysis with Edoardo Weiss, a pupil of Freud, which shaped his later poems on childhood."],
["What is the Libreria Antiquaria Umberto Saba?","His secondhand bookshop on Via San Nicolò in Trieste, bought in 1919, which still operates today."],
["What did Saba say poets must do?","In 'Quello che resta da fare ai poeti' (1911) he argued poets must write 'honest poetry', plain and truthful rather than decorative like D'Annunzio."]
],
"belli-sonnets":[
["How many Roman Sonnets did Belli write?","About 2,279 sonnets in the Roman dialect, mostly between 1830 and 1837, picturing the city's common people under papal rule."],
["Why did Belli write in dialect?","He wanted, he said, to leave a monument to the plebs of Rome, using their speech to record their beliefs, jokes and grievances."],
["Did Belli publish the sonnets?","No: he kept them secret, wanting them burned, but his son and friends preserved them; a full edition came out only after his death in 1863."],
["What do the sonnets say about the popes?","They mock the corruption and luxury of the papal court, like Pope Gregory XVI's love of wine, while showing common people's mix of faith and cynicism."],
["Who was Giuseppe Gioachino Belli?","A Roman poet and clerk (1791-1863), who later worked as a papal censor and opposed the 1849 Roman Republic."],
["Why is Belli sometimes compared to Balzac?","Like Balzac's Human Comedy, the sonnets portray an entire society, from cardinals to prostitutes, in vivid detail."],
["Who translated Belli into English?","Anthony Burgess translated selected sonnets in his novel ABBA ABBA (1977), and Mike Stocks published a large collection in 2007."],
["What is the statue of Belli in Rome?","A monument in Piazza Belli, Trastevere, unveiled in 1913, showing him in a top hat."],
["What topics do the sonnets cover?","Everything from the Last Judgment and Bible stories to prostitution, food, taxes and executions, often with obscene humour."]
],
"michelangelo-poems":[
["How many poems did Michelangelo write?","About 300 sonnets, madrigals and fragments, many unfinished, written from around 1503 until his death in 1564."],
["What did Michelangelo write about painting the Sistine Chapel?","A comic sonnet to Giovanni da Pistoia describing his neck bent back, beard pointing to heaven and paint dripping on his face, with a sketch of himself."],
["Who was Tommaso de' Cavalieri?","A young Roman nobleman Michelangelo met in 1532 and to whom he wrote passionate love poems and gave drawings like the Rape of Ganymede."],
["Who was Vittoria Colonna?","The Marchesa of Pescara, a poet and religious reformer, Michelangelo's close friend from about 1536 until her death in 1547, the subject of many spiritual sonnets."],
["What is Michelangelo's famous sonnet on sculpture?","'Non ha l'ottimo artista alcun concetto', saying the best artist has no idea that a block of marble does not already contain, which the hand must free."],
["Why were his poems altered?","His grandnephew Michelangelo the Younger published them in 1623, changing male pronouns to female to hide their homoerotic content."],
["When was the true text restored?","Cesare Guasti's edition of 1863 restored the original texts, and John Addington Symonds translated them into English in 1878."],
["What did Michelangelo write about Dante?","Two sonnets praising Dante as unjustly exiled by Florence, saying he would gladly suffer Dante's exile to possess his genius."],
["What did Michelangelo write at the end of his life?","Late sonnets reject art as an idol, saying neither painting nor sculpture can calm the soul turned to divine love."]
]});
QA({"foscolo-ortis":[
["What is The Last Letters of Jacopo Ortis about?","Jacopo Ortis, a young Venetian patriot betrayed when Napoleon hands Venice to Austria by the Treaty of Campo Formio in 1797, falls hopelessly in love with Teresa, who is promised to another, and kills himself."],
["What was the Treaty of Campo Formio?","Signed in October 1797, it ended the Venetian Republic's 1,100-year independence by ceding Venice to Austria in exchange for Belgium and Lombardy, a betrayal felt by Italian patriots."],
["Why is it compared to Werther?","Like Goethe's The Sorrows of Young Werther (1774), it is an epistolary novel about a sensitive young man in love with an engaged woman who commits suicide."],
["Who is Teresa engaged to?","Odoardo, a dull, rich man her father has chosen for financial reasons, while she loves Jacopo."],
["How does Jacopo die?","After a final visit to Teresa and his mother, he stabs himself in the heart with a dagger; he is found dying in the morning."],
["Who is Lorenzo Alderani?","Jacopo's friend, to whom the letters are addressed and who supposedly publishes them with comments after his death."],
["Who was Ugo Foscolo?","A poet born in 1778 on the Greek island of Zakynthos to a Venetian father, who fought in Napoleon's army and died poor in exile in London in 1827."],
["What is 'Dei sepolcri'?","Foscolo's 1807 poem 'On Tombs', arguing that tombs of great men inspire the living, written against Napoleon's edict banning burials in cities."],
["Where is Foscolo buried?","He was buried at Chiswick in London, but in 1871 his remains were moved to Santa Croce in Florence, near the tombs he praised in Dei sepolcri."],
["What does Jacopo say about Napoleon?","He condemns Napoleon as a tyrant who sold Venice, giving voice to Italian disillusionment with French promises of liberty."]
],
"nievo-confessions":[
["What is Confessions of an Italian about?","Carlino Altoviti, born a Venetian in 1775, tells his life up to age 83, from his childhood in the castle of Fratta in Friuli through Napoleon and the Risorgimento, becoming 'an Italian'."],
["What is the famous first sentence?","'I was born a Venetian on 18 October 1775, the day of St Luke the Evangelist, and I shall die by the grace of God an Italian'."],
["Who is La Pisana?","Carlino's capricious, passionate cousin and lifelong love, who marries an old man but nurses Carlino in exile in London and dies there of exhaustion."],
["What is the castle of Fratta?","A decaying feudal castle in Friuli where Carlino grows up as a poor relation, symbolizing the dying Venetian aristocracy."],
["Who was Ippolito Nievo?","A writer and patriot (1831-1861) who served with Garibaldi's Thousand in Sicily in 1860 as quartermaster, responsible for the expedition's accounts."],
["How did Nievo die?","He drowned in March 1861, aged 29, when the steamer Ercole sank in a storm in the Tyrrhenian Sea while he carried Garibaldi's financial records to Turin."],
["When was the novel published?","Written in about eight months in 1857-58, it was published posthumously in 1867 under the title Le confessioni di un ottuagenario."],
["How long is it?","Over 900 pages, one of the longest Italian novels of the 19th century."],
["What historical events appear?","The fall of Venice in 1797, the Parthenopean Republic in Naples in 1799, Napoleon's kingdom of Italy and the 1848 revolutions."],
["Why is it important for Italian identity?","It portrays the transformation of regional subjects into citizens of a unified nation, the core story of the Risorgimento."]
],
"deamicis-cuore":[
["What is Heart about?","A schoolboy's diary of his third-grade year (1881-82) in a Turin school, interspersed with monthly stories read in class and letters from his parents."],
["Who is the narrator?","Enrico Bottini, a middle-class boy whose diary records his classmates, teacher and the moral lessons of the year."],
["What are the 'monthly stories'?","Tales of heroic children from each region of Italy, like 'From the Apennines to the Andes', meant to unite young Italians."],
["What is 'From the Apennines to the Andes'?","The story of Marco, a Genoese boy who travels alone to Argentina to find his mother, who emigrated to work; it became the Japanese anime 3000 Leagues in Search of Mother (1976)."],
["Who is Franti?","The class bad boy, who laughs at everything, is expelled, and serves as the novel's villain; Umberto Eco later wrote an essay in praise of Franti."],
["Who is Garrone?","Enrico's big, kind classmate who protects the weak, the model of goodness."],
["What is 'The Little Patriot of Padua'?","A story about a poor boy who throws back coins given by foreigners after they insult Italy."],
["When was it published, and how successful was it?","It appeared on 18 October 1886, the first day of school, and went through 40 editions in two and a half months."],
["Who was Edmondo De Amicis?","A former army officer and journalist (1846-1908) from Liguria who later became a socialist."],
["Why is Cuore criticized today?","Critics find it excessively sentimental and nationalistic, with 'deamicisiano' becoming a word for tearful moralizing."]
]});
QA({"fontamara":[
["What does 'Fontamara' mean?","An invented village name in the Abruzzo, meaning 'bitter fountain', where poor peasants called 'cafoni' are cheated by landowners and Fascists."],
["What is a 'cafone'?","A southern Italian word for a peasant, also used as an insult meaning boor; Silone uses it with pride for the exploited villagers."],
["How is the village's water stolen?","The new podestà, a rich contractor called the Impresario, diverts the village stream to irrigate his own land, cheating the peasants with a sham agreement of 'three-quarters and three-quarters'."],
["Who is Berardo Viola?","A strong, rebellious young peasant who goes to Rome looking for work, is arrested, takes the blame for a clandestine anti-Fascist newspaper, and dies under torture in prison."],
["How does the novel end?","Inspired by Berardo's death, the villagers print a newspaper called 'What Is to Be Done?', and Fascist militia attack Fontamara, killing many; a few survivors flee abroad to tell the story."],
["When and where was it first published?","It was first published in German translation in Zurich in 1933, while Silone was in exile in Switzerland, and was published in Italy only after the war."],
["Who was Ignazio Silone?","The pen name of Secondino Tranquilli (1900-1978), from Pescina in Abruzzo, a founder of the Italian Communist Party expelled in 1931."],
["What is the Silone controversy?","In the 1990s historians found documents suggesting Silone was a police informer for the Fascist police in the 1920s, a claim still debated."],
["Why did Silone's village lose so many people in 1915?","The Avezzano earthquake of 13 January 1915 killed about 30,000 people, including Silone's mother and five of his brothers."],
["What is 'The God That Failed'?","A 1949 collection of essays by ex-Communists, including Silone, Arthur Koestler and Richard Wright, about their disillusionment."]
],
"vittorini-conversations":[
["What is Conversations in Sicily about?","Silvestro, a typesetter in Milan, gripped by 'abstract furies' in 1936, takes a train to visit his mother in Sicily, meeting strange characters on a journey that becomes a symbolic exploration."],
["What are the 'abstract furies'?","Silvestro's feeling of despair and powerlessness at the 'massacred world', an allusion to the Spanish Civil War and Fascism, which censors could not explicitly see."],
["Who does Silvestro meet on the journey?","The Great Lombard, a peasant who talks of 'other duties'; two policemen called Mustache and No-Mustache; and a knife-grinder who speaks of sharpening weapons."],
["Who is Concezione?","Silvestro's mother, a strong peasant woman who tells stories of his father and grandfather while giving injections to the sick in the village."],
["How does the novel end?","Silvestro learns his brother Liborio has been killed in war, talks with a bronze war-memorial statue, glimpses his father weeping at his mother's feet, and leaves without speaking to him."],
["When was it published?","It was serialized in 1938-39 in the magazine Letteratura, then published as a book in 1941, and later editions ran into trouble with Fascist censorship."],
["Who was Elio Vittorini?","A Sicilian writer (1908-1966), translator of American authors, and later editor of the magazine Il Politecnico and Einaudi's 'I gettoni' series."],
["What is 'Americana'?","Vittorini's 1941 anthology of American writing, censored by the Fascist regime, which helped make American literature popular in Italy."],
["Why did Vittorini reject The Leopard?","As an editor he turned it down in 1957 as old-fashioned, a decision often cited as one of publishing's great mistakes."],
["What does the father's Macbeth have to do with it?","Silvestro's father, a railway worker, was an amateur actor who played Macbeth, which his mother remembers with irony."]
],
"gadda-pasticciaccio":[
["What does 'Quer pasticciaccio brutto de via Merulana' mean?","'That nasty mess on Via Merulana', in Roman dialect; 'pasticciaccio' is a pejorative of 'pasticcio', a pie or mess."],
["What is the crime?","In March 1927 in Rome, the rich Countess Menegazzi's jewels are stolen, and days later Liliana Balducci is found brutally murdered in her flat in the same building."],
["Who is Ingravallo?","Don Ciccio Ingravallo, a Molisan police detective with philosophical views on causality, who believes every crime has a tangle of causes."],
["Who killed Liliana?","Gadda never says outright; the novel breaks off as Ingravallo confronts Assunta, Liliana's servant, who denies it, though hints suggest she or one of Liliana's protegées did it."],
["Why is the novel unfinished?","Gadda stopped as the investigation closes in, consistent with his belief that reality is an inextricable tangle of causes that no detective can fully unravel."],
["Why is it written in so many dialects?","Gadda mixes Roman, Neapolitan, Venetian and Molisan dialects with technical jargon and Latin, a linguistic carnival called 'plurilinguismo'."],
["When was it published?","Serialized in 1946 in the magazine Letteratura, then reworked into a book in 1957."],
["What does Gadda say about Mussolini?","The novel is set in Fascist Rome and contains satirical swipes at Mussolini, whom Gadda later attacked in his pamphlet Eros e Priapo."],
["Was it filmed?","Pietro Germi's Un maledetto imbroglio (1959) adapted it, with Germi himself as Ingravallo and Claudia Cardinale as Assunta."],
["Who was Carlo Emilio Gadda?","An engineer from Milan (1893-1973) who served in the First World War, was a prisoner of war, and wrote baroque, experimental prose."]
],
"arturos-island":[
["What is Arturo's Island about?","Arturo Gerace grows up almost alone on the island of Procida, near Naples, idolizing his absent father Wilhelm, until his father brings home a 16-year-old bride, Nunziata."],
["Who is Wilhelm Gerace?","Arturo's half-German father, a handsome, mysterious man who spends most of his time away; Arturo imagines him a hero, but discovers he is in love with a male convict."],
["Who is Nunziata?","Arturo's young stepmother from Naples, devout and simple, whom Arturo comes to love; she rejects him when he kisses her after she gives birth."],
["Who is Tonino Stella?","A young prisoner at the Procida penitentiary whom Wilhelm loves; he calls Wilhelm 'parodia' (parody), shattering Arturo's illusions."],
["How does the novel end?","Arturo leaves Procida at 16 to join the army as the Second World War begins, refusing to look back at the island as his boat departs."],
["What prize did it win?","It won the Strega Prize in 1957, the first woman to win it."],
["What was Procida's prison?","The Palazzo d'Avalos, a former royal palace used as a penitentiary from 1830 to 1988."],
["Who made the film?","Damiano Damiani's 1962 film L'isola di Arturo, which won a prize at San Sebastián."],
["What does Arturo's name mean to him?","He believes it comes from the star Arcturus, and is proud to share a name with King Arthur."]
],
"cosmicomics":[
["What are Cosmicomics?","Twelve stories (1965) by Italo Calvino, each beginning with a scientific fact about the universe and narrated by Qfwfq, an ageless being who witnessed it all."],
["Who is Qfwfq?","An ageless narrator with an unpronounceable palindromic name, who has been a mollusc, a dinosaur, a particle and more."],
["What is 'The Distance of the Moon'?","When the Moon was close to Earth, Qfwfq and others climbed up to it on ladders to harvest moon-milk; the deaf cousin loves the Moon, and Qfwfq's love, Mrs Vhd Vhd, stays there as it drifts away."],
["What is 'All at One Point'?","Before the Big Bang, everyone lived crammed into a single point; Mrs Ph(i)Nko's wish to make noodles for everyone creates space and the universe."],
["What is 'The Dinosaurs'?","Qfwfq, the last dinosaur, lives among the new creatures who fear dinosaurs as legends, but don't recognize him as one."],
["What is 'A Sign in Space'?","Qfwfq makes a sign in space as the galaxy rotates, to find it again after 200 million years; it has been erased and replaced by others."],
["How did Calvino come up with Cosmicomics?","Calvino read popular science and was inspired by the idea that mythic stories could be created out of science, like cartoons of the cosmos."],
["Did Calvino write more of them?","Yes: t zero (1967), and Cosmicomiche vecchie e nuove (1984); a complete English edition was published in 2009."],
["What prize did Cosmicomics win in English?","William Weaver's translation won the National Book Award for Translation in 1969."],
["What is 'Without Colours'?","Qfwfq describes Earth before it had an atmosphere, when everything was grey, and his love Ayl, who flees into the colourless depths when colours arrive."]
],
"deledda-reeds":[
["What is Reeds in the Wind about?","Efix, an old servant of the impoverished Pintor sisters in Sardinia, tends their last field while hiding a guilty secret, as their nephew Giacinto arrives and brings ruin."],
["What is Efix's secret?","Years before, he accidentally killed the sisters' tyrannical father, Don Zame, when helping the youngest sister Lia escape to the mainland."],
["Why are humans 'reeds in the wind'?","Efix says people are like reeds bent by the wind of fate, unable to resist the forces of destiny."],
["Who is Giacinto?","Lia's son from the mainland, who comes to the village and runs up debts with the usurer Kallina, threatening the sisters' property."],
["How does the novel end?","Efix becomes a wandering beggar in penance, then returns to see Giacinto married to the poor girl Grixenda and Noemi to her rich cousin Don Predu, and dies peacefully."],
["Where is the novel set?","In Galte, a fictional version of Galtellì in the Baronie region of eastern Sardinia, along the river Cedrino."],
["When did Grazia Deledda win the Nobel?","In 1926 (awarded in 1927), the first and so far only Italian woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature."],
["Who was Grazia Deledda?","A writer from Nuoro, Sardinia (1871-1936), largely self-taught, who moved to Rome after marrying and wrote over 30 novels."],
["What are the 'panas' and 'janas'?","Sardinian folklore spirits: 'panas' are ghosts of women who died in childbirth, 'janas' small fairies living in prehistoric rock tombs."],
["When was it published?","Serialized in 1913 in the magazine Nuova Antologia, then as a book the same year."]
]});
